window.harnessChunks["rows-9527297488befbea.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-06T22:14:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jx-grxf/PatchPilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PatchPilot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-06\">2026-11-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jx-grxf/PatchPilot/tree/79eed49aa6c8daf7dd939047031c942424782abc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 79eed49aa6c8daf7dd939047031c942424782abc\">79eed49 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce code/repository-work outcomes through one or more environment-facing model-directed observe–act–observe coding loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code structure, heterogeneous user tasks, unexpected tool output, tool/model errors, context limits and discovered test results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next concrete tool invocation, child task or correction after observations; decide whether a coding answer is complete. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed AgentRunner, and bounded model-backed child loops for delegated local operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model capability probing, tool schemas and repair, WorkspaceTools, approval mode, command/path policy, child allowlists, cancellation, context compaction and session events. - Closure path: user task and code observations → model-selected tool/task → validated/approved first-party execution → returned result or error → revised model action or answer. - Boundary reachability: shipped CLI/TUI starts the main loop; the optional shipped task path constructs real child runners without importing an external agent framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actors, deterministic tools and permissions would not choose task-specific edits, exploration or iterative correction. Those choices remain agent-discretionary within bounded operator rights. - Evidence: agent.ts; subagentRunner.ts; workspace.ts; toolSchema.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the selected model inference endpoint must be available; writes or shell steps may be blocked pending a human decision. Child loops exist only in configured or explicitly requested delegation mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no specific inter-S1 disturbance plus a closed coordinating response is established at this task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the main loop and optional children can touch one workspace, but no demonstrated cross-child write collision, negotiation or oscillation with a dedicated mitigation/feedback relation is shown. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent-owned cross-S1 coordination decision evidenced. - Decision owner: not established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent task requests, child-specific tool permissions, serial child execution, bounded steps and compact parent-facing results. - Closure path: child results inform the parent task loop; serial execution is explicitly designed for prefix-cache preservation, not evidenced regulation of interference among autonomous S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent delegation and serial scheduling organize work but do not identify a concrete inter-S1 disturbance and adaptive coordination closure required by the Profile. - Evidence: agent.ts child scheduling; subagentRunner.ts; architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: optional child editing is genuine S1 delegation; a later implementation of conflict detection, shared state reservations or cross-child feedback could change this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current performance/resource bargaining across distinct operational units is established beyond one task&#39;s orchestration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool failures, step count, permission risk and task progress are controlled locally, not demonstrated as competing commitments or portfolio-level performance requiring S3 intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model can select subtask assignments, but no evidenced whole-system resource/priority intervention authority distinct from task decomposition. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child step cap, serial invocation, todo list, result envelopes, permission mode, cancellation and loop breaker. - Closure path: child output flows to the parent model and can change the next coding step; no separate current-operation-wide allocation or exception-regulation cycle is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task delegation and bounded execution are real, but parent summarization and static limits are insufficient for an S3 organizational claim. - Evidence: agent.ts; subagentRunner.ts; toolSchema.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the parent can react to a failing child; a task-local retry or delegation decision should not be mislabeled current-system accountability across organizational units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary inspection channel that challenges routine agent claims and returns audit findings to a distinct supervisory decision path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: output errors may be caught by tests or viewed in /diff, but these are ordinary task verification and operator inspection rather than an independent audit of S1/S3 reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate auditor with independent access and corrective supervisory return evidenced. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary run-tests/shell results, Git diff, terminal telemetry, JSONL logs, child transcripts and optional same-agent post-write verification nudge. - Closure path: operational tool results feed the same model loop and user view; no distinct independent auditor&#39;s finding is routed to supervisory correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a child explore agent may independently read files, but it is delegated operational research—not a deployed complementary audit path for the producer&#39;s claims. - Evidence: agent.ts verification and child results; session.ts; subagentRunner.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this does not deny the utility of normal tests, file inspection or the human&#39;s own independent review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned prospective environmental intelligence to future capability adaptation and S3 dialogue is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing local runtimes, model availability, long-session context and version releases are monitored or configured for current use, not turned into a prospective organizational capability strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous future-option formation and approved capability-change return demonstrated. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime/model discovery, /connect, /update with user confirmation, context compaction and optional memory persistence. - Closure path: operator selects models or installs a release and context history influences task continuation; no first-party environment-sensing → future-option → capability-change → present-control conversation is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: endpoint enumeration and upgrade prompts are operational configuration, while generated task plans target present coding work, not future system adaptation. - Evidence: localRuntimes.ts; contextStore.ts; memory.ts; tui/commands.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a future agent-directed adaptation layer would require fresh review at its actual deployment boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/mission or parent-governed policy adjudication with durable return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local workspace risks, shell commands, hidden files, permissions and prompt behavior are limited by static code and human action-level approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5 identity-level policy choice, legitimacy escalation or return governing later operations. - Decision owner: not established; human operator chooses modes and accepts/denies individual side effects. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan/build/bypass, tool allowlists, workspace confinement, sensitive path denial, shell risk classification and approval handler. - Closure path: a requested write/shell action is allowed or denied for the current task. This local permission does not enact a new organizational identity/policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves the authored safety rules and human authorization intact; the model is not delegated ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: workspace.ts; agent.ts prompt/mode; product-context.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval is legitimate local action authorization; this is not evidence of a distinct S5 organizational policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a589498adad37ee06a29ea055e38512378be9fc5/assessments/pa/patchpilot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a589498adad37ee06a29ea055e38512378be9fc5\">a589498 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/esengine/DeepSeek-Reasonix\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reasonix</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/esengine/DeepSeek-Reasonix/tree/ce278545461225dfd4c77840ad2a756bfb2095b9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ce278545461225dfd4c77840ad2a756bfb2095b9\">ce27854 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Reasonix owns an autonomous coding-agent loop that plans and executes tool actions against the workspace.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: C. Concurrent subagents use explicit write-path claims: disjoint claims can run concurrently, overlapping/omitted claims serialize, and whole-workspace or broader tool access escalates the claim. This directly attenuates concurrent workspace mutation collisions. The mechanism is runtime/constructor-owned rather than an autonomous S2 actor.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. Planner/executor composition, checkpoints and permission machinery structure current execution but do not establish a distinct first-party whole-system regulator owning shared organizational priorities/resources/commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Review/security labels and ordinary verification surfaces do not establish a materially independent audit actor with distinct evidence access and corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. No separate outside-looking prospective capability-adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Permissions and workspace policy constrain actions but do not close organizational identity or ultimate-policy tension.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/reasonix.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T21:13:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sjMun09/Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sjMun09/Harness/tree/c1bc066f5608167790d229f818d989a15a265ad4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c1bc066f5608167790d229f818d989a15a265ad4\">c1bc066 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven coding file/shell operations and delegated codebase investigation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task variety, source changes and tool/error responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tool/action after seeing model and tool results. - Decision owner: Root model-guided coding agent; optional read-only child agent when spawned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool map, retry, permission, context compaction, bounded-turn guard. - Closure path: selected tool → dispatch → tool result appended → model chooses next operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Kernel alone does not select task-relevant source edits. - Evidence: crates/harness-core/src/engine.rs, crates/harness-cli/src/subagenthost.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded frozen first-party source, no empirical capability claims. - Boundary reachability: CLI constructs first-party Rust runturn and tool context; child host is reachable in supported CLI operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced distinctive interaction-induced S1 interference regulation and return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Several tool calls may run concurrently, but they are actions inside one S1 session, not proof of interacting autonomous S1 operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent delegates child investigation; dispatcher gathers outputs without regulating child-versus-child conflicts. - Decision owner: Task model for delegation, runtime for depth/allowlist/dispatch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only subagent tools, child cancellation, async joinall. - Closure path: Child output reaches parent as a tool result, not an inter-S1 coordination corrective response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic parallel tool dispatch and read-only bounds do not establish S2. - Evidence: crates/harness-core/src/engine.rs, crates/harness-core/src/subagent.rs, crates/harness-cli/src/subagenthost.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system discretionary authority over currently competing S1 commitments/resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current token budgets, tool risk, planning and provider failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator-defined limits and permission outcomes govern local operation, not shared operational priorities. - Decision owner: Operator for config, deterministic runtime for enforcement; root model for coding task decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan gate, hooks, permission snapshot, transactions and retries. - Closure path: Per-operation reject/retry/rollback affects local execution; no returned S3 whole-current control decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A configured gate cannot claim ownership of the decisions it enforces. - Evidence: crates/harness-core/src/engine.rs, crates/harness-core/src/plangate.rs, crates/harness-core/src/tx.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently judged complementary audit over routine claims with consequential corrective closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False completion or untested file changes may be detected by routine tools. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Test reminders, external hook scripts and logs can support a check but do not own a separate audit finding. - Decision owner: Operator scripts and deterministic plan gate, not a first-party independent auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HARNESS.md verification reminders, CI/testing, event streams, child JSONL logs. - Closure path: Test/permission outcomes feed task context; no complementary audit → authoritative returned correction pathway. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Normal test prompts, review labels and lifecycle hooks alone do not establish S3. - Evidence: crates/harness-core/src/plangate.rs, crates/harness-core/src/hooks.rs, crates/harness-cli/src/subagenthost.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No externally prospective future capability-adaptation decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing tools/models and future conditions beyond the current coding task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compaction, provider retry and project guidance update current execution only. - Decision owner: Operator config and root task model; no future-oriented adaptation owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session memory, compactor, hooks, provider failover/backoff. - Closure path: Immediate task turns continue; no external future option generates an installed capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: State trimming/retry is not S4. - Evidence: crates/harness-core/src/engine.rs, crates/harness-core/src/memory.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate policy/identity deliberation → legitimate authority → binding return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Security/trust conflicts, unsafe tools, project policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators select permission/plan/hook policy and confirm calls. - Decision owner: External user/developer for policy, deterministic runtime for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionSnapshot, HookDispatcher, prompt and trust mode. - Closure path: Tool requests allowed/denied, with no identity-level decision return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A static policy file and per-task confirmation do not establish S5. - Evidence: crates/harness-core/src/engine.rs, crates/harness-core/src/hooks.rs, crates/harness-cli/src/trust.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3290dbadb0563ec3ad4f34e09b65e0825893400c/assessments/sjmun09-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3290dbadb0563ec3ad4f34e09b65e0825893400c\">3290dba &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-06T23:04:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonathanhecl/vibe-coder\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">vibe-coder</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-06\">2026-11-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonathanhecl/vibe-coder/tree/49f6598b756b3ec9440c80f16bdd556624a152f1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 49f6598b756b3ec9440c80f16bdd556624a152f1\">49f6598 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository/code operations with a real iterative model-selected tool–observation–correction loop, including separately reasoning delegated child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing source context, failing tests, ambiguous file locations, incomplete requirements, tool errors, multi-step mission progress and child task findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which task-specific file, shell, web or child action to attempt next and adapt after receiving concrete output. - Decision owner: configured main model acting through Agent.Run and bounded model-backed SubAgent instances for their local tool decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, Ollama native/XML support, retry/cap guards, file readback/auto-test, permissions, context compaction, durable checklist/mission and sub-agent scope caps. - Closure path: developer goal → model-selected tool or delegated child → authorized action on current project → real observation → model revision/continuation or final response. - Boundary reachability: first-party CLI/REPL calls Agent.Run; first-party registry exposes SubAgent/ParallelAgents; their model calls and tools operate within the product runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model eliminates the task-specific choice and subsequent feedback response; rule-only tools and the REPL do not select edits independently. - Evidence: agent/run.go; tools/subagent.go; cmd/vibe/repl.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: local inference must be configured; delegated writers need parent permission and their own concrete tool outcomes can still conflict at a higher workflow granularity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a bounded concurrent-child disturbance by ensuring one delegated concrete tool action, including permission/checkpoint/test handling, executes at a time against the shared parent runtime. - Distinct S1 units: at least two concurrently progressing fresh-context model-backed SubAgent loops launched by ParallelAgents, each able to choose permitted Read/Glob/Grep and optional Write/Edit/Bash actions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent child tools would otherwise overlap shared permission prompts, first-party Git checkpoints, auto-test execution and shared worktree file mutations. Those effects can collide or be observed at unstable partial states. - Attenuating coordination relation: Agent.executeDelegatedTool wraps each delegated action under one parent-owned delegatedMu and routes it through the same permission/mode/checkpoint executor; ParallelAgents runs model loops concurrently while delegated concrete tool effects are mutually exclusive. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each child waits for its authorized action to finish and consumes that action&#39;s resulting output before selecting another, then reports its findings to the spawner. Serialized tool effects prevent simultaneous execution of child actions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mutex is a concrete anti-overlap critical section for actual shared-workspace side effects from distinct autonomous child loops, not merely a task dispatcher or notification channel. Its reach is deliberately per delegated tool action, not a multi-step conflict-free transaction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: intersecting tool side effects and the permission/checkpoint/test state they share during parallel subordinate operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a parent model decides parallel delegation and tool intents; the first-party executor determines the non-overlapping execution order and enforces parent permissions, irrespective of child preference. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party parent executor and mutex, not an independently negotiating coordinator; hence constructor-level C, not autonomous S2 A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: errgroup parallel child scheduling, shared parent executor injection, delegatedMu, tool policy/authorization, checkpoint and auto-test invocation. - Closure path: parallel child intents → one child holds the delegated-tool lock → parent-approved effect completes → child observes output and continues → next child obtains lock, avoiding concurrent tool execution. - Boundary reachability: ParallelAgents.Execute concurrently invokes SubAgent.runLoop; runLoop uses executorFromContext, installed by the main agent; executeDelegatedTool locks and calls first-party executeTool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the serialized critical section works without a model deciding coordination policy. It reduces a real child–child tool-interference mode, but cannot stop a later tool from overwriting a sibling&#39;s earlier edit. - Evidence: tools/subagent.go; agent/toolpolicy.go; agent/delegationtest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural; no dedicated concurrent-writer loss-prevention test is claimed. - Confidence: medium-high for tool-level S2; limited for workflow-level interference. - Caveats: no per-child Git worktree, transaction-level mutual exclusion, changed-file reconciliation or guaranteed prevention of sequential lost updates. The coordination claim is restricted to synchronized delegated tool executions and shared helper state.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current operational management authority over an aggregate of independently operating S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the current mission and delegated subtask completion, iterations, retries, permission refusals and tool output are managed locally rather than across a broader operating portfolio. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separately closed current-resource allocation, cross-worker performance correction or supervisor arbitration right beyond the initiating coding conversation&#39;s work. - Decision owner: not established at S3 recursion; the spawner handles task-level delegation and the CLI enforces deterministic caps/stall policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TODO/TaskStart/TaskComplete, mission progress, bounded children, JEV reported completion, execution counters, persistent logs and CLI stall guards. - Closure path: individual task feedback and mission events return to the same coding agent or human; no separately evidenced whole-current S3 intervention loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: mission ownership and delegated task orchestration are S1 task continuation; generic counters, logs and permission limits do not show an authority to govern an operating portfolio. - Evidence: agent/mission.go; tools/mission.go; cmd/vibe/repl.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: current-task supervisory-looking behavior exists; it does not warrant extrapolation to organizational S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no demonstrably independent reality-checking complementary audit of S1 output with a separate corrective supervisory closure exists. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion claims, failed commands, risky actions and incorrect edits are checked through the ordinary tool/test loop and optional JEV second-opinion classification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: JEV can flag reported mission progress as incomplete and inject a reminder, but cannot independently inspect raw files, run audit tests or veto an agent&#39;s direct MissionComplete call; no independent evidence/return right is established. - Decision owner: main model still owns mission completion; JEV is a separate configured classifier over a supplied goal and session-progress text, not an independently mandated S3 auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewMissionCompletion, CheckGoalCompletion, explicit session reminder, optional JevDecide, auto-test, readback and saved JSONL run log. - Closure path: current mission&#39;s goal + progress/session messages → optional JEV decision → reminder to the main agent on incomplete result; it may continue ordinary task work. There is no separately governed direct-source inspection → independent finding → enforced supervisory correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: JEV performs a real discrete model judgment, but the observations are selected from the operating session; its separate model identity alone does not prove independence of evidence or of audit authority. - Evidence: agent/mission.go; jevstyle/jevstyle.go; cmd/vibe/repl.go; missionjevfeedbacktest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this negative label does not deny JEV&#39;s independent model call or useful completion feedback; it denies that the narrower complementarily independent audit closure is established at this frozen ref.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no independently closed prospective environmental scan and future-capability adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider availability, network search results, repository changes and context pressure are handled for current tasks or through user-configured extensions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent-controlled future organizational option, investment or capability-change proposal returned to current control. - Decision owner: not established; user configures models/MCP/skills, while the agent uses those as present-task resources. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WebSearch/WebFetch/HTTPRequest, optional RAG and sidecar, model/endpoint selection and MCP/skills configuration. - Closure path: user-supplied or retrieved facts inform current work; no future-threat/option assessment and accountable adoption/rollback into the operating organization is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: looking up web facts and adapting a coding plan are S1 activities, not continuing strategic S4 intelligence. - Evidence: README.md; agent/run.go; agent/agent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: optional external MCP agents are not silently imported as first-party S4 functions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate organizational ultimate-purpose/identity policy decision and binding policy feedback loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: protected file operations, dangerous shell patterns, web access, child permissions and unattended mission risk are constrained through static code and operator settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the user configures permissions; mandatory dangerous actions remain human-guarded/denied. Neither the main agent nor JEV is authorized to redefine the parent mission or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: no S5 owner within the assessed harness; operator and authored constraints retain ordinary authorization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission tiers, remembered allow/deny, mandatory command denials, plan/review restrictions, JEV optional assisted-risk classification, sanitized subprocess environment and inherited child ceilings. - Closure path: attempted tool effect → permission/mode/danger gate → allow/deny or user approval → individual action proceeds/halts. No ultimate policy issue → parent ratification → durable identity-policy change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action-level risk decisions may be assisted by JEV and autonomous missions may run unattended, but neither determines the legitimate constitutional identity or self-authorizes changed governance rules. - Evidence: permissions/manager.go; agent/toolpolicy.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: autonomous execution and delegated risk screening increase operational power without transferring identity-policy authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae1af8f65f279bfa11156670d510d519d961d7e2/assessments/vi/vibe-coder.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae1af8f65f279bfa11156670d510d519d961d7e2\">ae1af8f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T21:21:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GembaCore/gemba-core\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gemba</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GembaCore/gemba-core/tree/64c266f889c6d6a579904c5e336676fa1c49b216\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 64c266f889c6d6a579904c5e336676fa1c49b216\">64c266f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: the in-process Onboarder adaptively transforms a user&#39;s project request and conversational feedback into a validated milestone/epic/bead work plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or changing operator requirements, plan-schema errors, inconsistencies between prior draft state and a new message. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose revised project structure and conversational response from prior state/new request; choose a corrected plan after validation feedback. The consequential write/ratification right remains human-owned. - Decision owner: the model-driven Onboarder constructed/wired by Gemba&#39;s first-party skill and LLM transport; a vendor API supplies the model inference, not a prebuilt external agent harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: newproject.Run, model client, strict envelope/schema validation, retry-once, session Turn state, operator ratifier. - Closure path: user message + prior state → model-composed plan/reply → first-party validation/retry → next draft state and further operator feedback; separately, explicit ratification creates project work. - Boundary reachability: gemba serve installs the real Onboarder SkillTurner and NewRatifier; a configured client enables this first-party route. A Beads-only mode without an LLM has reduced scope and does not demonstrate it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the installed model chooses the plan contents from conversational feedback rather than a fixed template or external coding CLI. The model does not own final project authorization, downstream coding or a generic autonomous tool-action loop. - Evidence: internal/cli/serve.go; internal/skills/newproject/skill.go; internal/personas/onboarder/onboarder.go; internal/server/newproject.go; internal/server/newprojectratify.go. - Basis: explicit installed source path and structural closure; no provider-backed live run. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded planning S1; not a claim of first-party autonomous software engineering. - Caveats: this S1 is specifically conversational project-plan production. Native worker processes remain independent autonomous systems.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent two managed external worker sessions from consuming conflicting current repository/worktree work while the configured pool allocates ready jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent task assignment to sessions targeting overlapping Git repository/branch/worktree resources; duplicate claims and unsafe reuse. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject a conflicting ready bead, choose a non-conflicting eligible bead, and dispatch or decline/cold-start a session under configured resource gates. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic scheduler/collision constructor operating under user-provided pool configuration; not the attached worker models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkspaceCollisions, daemon ready/live/idle view, DispatchGate, SessionDispatcher, first-party native StartSession, atomic claim semantics. - Closure path: live worker assignments + ready-bead targets → workspace collision edges and pool eligibility → incompatible assignments withheld, eligible assignment claimed/dispatched → subsequent worker sessions operate on non-conflicting selected targets; later pool ticks refresh current occupancy. - Boundary reachability: first-party startPoolDaemons runs after production serve config resolves pools, wiring the daemon to the installed native orchestration and WorkPlane adapters. It is opt-in, not inherent in bare Beads-only mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Gemba constructs the specific interference attenuator and enforces it automatically; the configured policy and scheduler, rather than an autonomous model negotiating conflicts, own the decision. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently operating agent sessions launched/adopted under Gemba&#39;s native OrchestrationPlane; individual coding-agent model loops remain external dependencies. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping worker writes to the same repository/branch/worktree and duplicated task claim across concurrently active sessions. - Attenuating coordination relation: worktree-target collision graph and atomic current-bead claim before StartSession/dispatch, plus configured max-concurrency and minimum dispatch intervals. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: collision gate removes candidate assignments before launch, then updated active/idle session state and claims shape the following scheduling cycle. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: gate inspects contested writable resource targets of multiple operational sessions and withholds overlapping execution, directly attenuating inter-worker interference rather than merely displaying a queue. - Evidence: internal/cli/poolwiring.go; internal/planner/autodispatch/daemon.go; internal/planner/workspacecollision.go; internal/planner/selection/selection.go; internal/adapter/native/start.go. - Basis: wired, selectable native operating mode; structural interference suppression. - Confidence: medium-high for the opt-in construction, not an observed live concurrency outcome. - Caveats: Gemba prevents specific resource collisions; it cannot guarantee semantic noninterference or correctness of agent-produced code.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent operator&#39;s current workspace-wide choice of live commitments, work priority and explicit launch/redispatch across managed work items and active sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing work commitments, blocked dependencies, operator attention and need to reallocate current agent execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect aggregate project/work and session state, edit the active work graph and explicitly start eligible epic/milestone descendant jobs; the autonomous pool scorer does not independently choose organizational mission or portfolio strategy. - Decision owner: human project owner/operator using Gemba&#39;s first-party board and task/dispatch API; automatic scheduler only enforces configured bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkPlane state, board and work-item edit routes, dependency selection, native OrchestrationPlane StartSession, work-item cascade dispatcher, status/attention reporting. - Closure path: project board and worker/task state → operator edits priorities/assignments or submits dispatch → Gemba applies current work mutation / starts permitted child sessions → updated WorkPlane and active session state feed back to subsequent operator and pool decisions. - Boundary reachability: shipped HTTP/SPA and POST /api/work-items/{id}/cascade-dispatch with native adaptor (rather than merely a design document); Beads-only read-only mode cannot perform this closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human parent retains judgment over current portfolio commitments, while Gemba executes constrained requests. Deterministic affinity and auto-launch do not warrant autonomous S3 ownership. - Whole-system current view: per-project active work graph/board and available session statuses, including ready/blocker/assignment and action results; this is a project/workspace view, not arbitrary cross-tenant coordination. - Current-control decision scope: manual work-item mutation, active epic/milestone dispatch and choice to launch new worker commitments; not only reading logs or approving isolated tools. - Evidence: README.md; internal/server/workitemscascade.go; internal/server/workitemswrite.go; internal/adapter/native/start.go; internal/cli/serve.go. - Basis: explicit user-present control and first-party effects route. - Confidence: medium for the supported parent-owned workflow; no specific operator intervention was run live. - Caveats: scope is one workspace/project; operator decisions outside Gemba or a broader organization are not imported.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no reachable first-party complementary audit owner with materially independent operational access and findings-to-current-control closure is established in the pinned supported modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker completion claims, QA correctness, declared-vs-actual modification drift and escalation findings are plausible audit subjects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent challenge of ordinary S1/S3 operational claims based on separate observed evidence, with findings causing an actual later correction or control adjustment. Not furnished end-to-end. - Decision owner: none identified for an independent audit function within the credited Gemba runtime; the operator reviews escalations, and optional persona suggestions depend on external Claude Code consultations rather than an installed complementary auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: walk agenda, persona consult audit log, bootstrapreview advisory batch review, regression suite registry, offline retrospective comparator and evidence adapters. - Closure path: the shipped walk lists current work and escalations for an operator, and persona recommendations may be applied by operator action, but no separately sourced independent completion audit regularly or optionally reaches binding corrective operations in the evidenced first-party standard mode. - Boundary reachability: serve wires AttachWalk, but Witness/Refinery sources are nil/no-op; the consult-inside-walk handler returns 501, and qa/regression explicitly registers potential suites without executing them. retro.Runner has logic to compare actual Git diffs with declarations and update profiles but is not wired into serve by the reviewed production code; gemba retro list only reads existing/offline grades. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither advisory user-led review nor an unwired retrospective or future witness adapter grants a Gemba first-party independent audit actor a decisive judgment-and-return right. External vendor agent reasoning is not credited. - Evidence: internal/cli/serve.go; internal/server/walks.go; internal/walk/sources/witness.go; internal/walk/sources/refinery.go; internal/qa/regression/suite.go; internal/planner/retro/runner.go; internal/cli/retro.go; internal/skills/bootstrapreview/skill.go. - Basis: explicit non-execution/no-op/501 production paths plus structural comparison of possible audit sources. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded shipped-mode absence; does not rule out operator-attached independent review integrations outside the pinned standard distribution. - Caveats: if an externally integrated worker/witness supplies its own independent audit, it belongs to that enlarged composed system, not to Gemba by inference.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported Gemba-owned external-and-prospective intelligence/adaptation loop with a new capability option and returned installation into current operating capacity is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incoming project demands, changing technological environment, worker effectiveness, evolving dependencies and future requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently or parent-governedly recognize future/environment distinctions and choose/install an adaptation of organizational capability. No such function-specific path is evidenced. - Decision owner: the operator may change task strategy and configuration; onboarder/PM personas may propose milestones and epic order. These are current/project planning and operator decisions, not demonstrated outside-and-then capability governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PM epicorder, project Onboarder, source scan scheduling, agent-profile scoring and retrospective comparison/calibration. - Closure path: future milestone plans and current work rankings can enter WorkPlane and affect today’s task order, but no outside-world forecast/option development → capability adaptation decision → updated Gemba operational repertoire was found. - Boundary reachability: shipped Onboarder and PM skill are wired, but their prompts are scoped to task plan/sprint-order; source scan scheduler is a deterministic trigger/coalescer, and retro runner is not wired into production serve. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: bounded planning about later tasks and updated affinity scores are not an organizational future/environment intelligence process. External model or developer modification is not attributed to Gemba. - Evidence: internal/skills/newproject/skill.go; internal/server/embeddedpersonas/project-manager.toml; internal/skills/epicorder/skill.go; internal/planner/scanscheduler/scheduler.go; internal/planner/retro/runner.go; internal/cli/serve.go. - Basis: first-party role and production-wire scrutiny, explicit distinction of prospective capability vs current-task planning. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded present distribution, not universal impossibility. - Caveats: future extensions may provide environment intelligence or evolutionary selection; unshipped/injected components are outside the frozen scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-identity or ethos/policy regulatory decision with appropriate authority and return to whole-system operation is demonstrated beyond project setup. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fundamental organizational mission, operating identity and tensions between current commitments and future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish/revise identity or ultimate policy, resolve S3–S4-level tensions and govern subsequent operations. Not evidenced for the assessed deployed harness. - Decision owner: a human project user selects and ratifies the initial project plan; repository developers/operators own app configuration. Neither is shown making a function-specific ultimate-policy decision through a shipped Gemba identity/governance loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Onboarder validation and separate project-ratification transaction, workspace configs, permission flags, credentials, tenant/auth layer, static persona role descriptions. - Closure path: model-proposed project draft → user authorizes creation → directory/WorkPlane is initialized. This is operative project creation and does not exhibit identity/ultimate-policy choice after environmental adaptation tension or a later policy return that governs the organizational whole. - Boundary reachability: the initial project ratifier is production-wired, but no S5-specific identity-policy channel and subsequent policy-effect loop is evidenced in the audited surface. This is not a negative claim about human ultimate authority outside Gemba. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM plan construction, human consent and static enforcement do not turn the model, ratifier or permission checks into an S5 organ. A parent-owned S5 P needs a real policy-level decision route, not only a normal project-creation confirmation. - Evidence: internal/server/newprojectratify.go; internal/cli/serve.go; internal/personas/onboarder/onboarder.go; internal/server/newproject.go; internal/auth/. - Basis: source-confirmed setup/authorization boundary; no distinct S5 policy closure in shipped runtime. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded reviewed first-party modes. - Caveats: a separate institution or user can hold S5 for a wider organization that deploys Gemba; that wider owner is outside this repository-relative assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f2d78679f7c6cf6115124bf81042bd387e5d7efa/assessments/gemba.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f2d78679f7c6cf6115124bf81042bd387e5d7efa\">f2d7867 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T16:03:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/twaldin/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">twaldin/harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/twaldin/harness/tree/f75dee4fd8f64d9357fb2862741f87ddb313250d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f75dee4fd8f64d9357fb2862741f87ddb313250d\">f75dee4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform a caller&#39;s coding-agent task through a supported installed autonomous CLI/RPC/SDK agent and return output or a retained turn result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task instructions, project files, tool feedback and agent process/session outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the invoked native agent selects task-specific reasoning and tool actions, while the caller selects which agent and capability/permission envelope to activate. - Decision owner: selected external AI coding agent inside the supported assembled run; not harness adapter dispatch, parser or subprocess code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed spec validation, argv construction, instruction projection, owned process groups, bounded output, cancellation/timeouts, identity checks, and session event transport. - Closure path: caller prompt/workdir → launched or connected coding-agent runtime → agent&#39;s own tool/result cycle → run output or live session result returned to caller; a retained session can accept follow-up turns. - Boundary reachability: the shipped run and opensession pathways actually launch/connect to supported agent backends; this does not rely on example-only actors, and the required separately installed agent is an explicit supported runtime dependency. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-specific actions cannot be selected by the remaining adapter/parser if the native agent&#39;s model discretion is removed; the library only mediates launch and transport. - Evidence: registry run, adapter execution, session entry, supported modes. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for an assembled supported agent mode, not task success. - Caveats: harness neither bundles nor controls the third-party agent&#39;s internal decision logic; its one-shot command builder alone would not establish S1 autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no supported first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop is evidenced at one run/session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workdir projection leases, process teardown and concurrent caller resource use are host safety concerns, not evidenced oscillation of multiple autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: external caller coordinates separate agent sessions, if any; Harness only owns per-session resource safety. - Decision owner: caller for cross-session orchestration; deterministic library for individual lease/cleanup enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workdir leases, process groups, session start/close and identity checks. - Closure path: lease and lifecycle rules prevent local ownership conflicts but no S2-specific decision from one operating unit changes the other independent S1 unit&#39;s subsequent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: many available adapter types and sessions are choices of backend, not a first-party organization of mutually adjusting S1 agents. - Evidence: session lifecycle, subprocess lifecycle, shared contract. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for documented supported distribution. - Caveats: an application built on the library may implement coordination, but its S1 units, collision response and owner must be assessed separately.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current portfolio regulation with discretionary resource/commitment decisions is shipped for this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one run&#39;s timeout, output budget, cancellation, and permission settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller chooses permissions, model, agent and runtime limits; deterministic code applies them. - Decision owner: external caller for policy and run configuration, native agent for task actions, not an in-boundary S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RunSpec, backend validation, process termination, session open/close and output limits. - Closure path: runtime stops/returns a failed or completed run; no governing whole-system reprioritization or resource bargain is decided and returned to independent S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: stopping a process or enforcing a preselected timeout is not agent ownership of a common current-control choice. - Evidence: RunSpec/caller settings, run and cleanup, process controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: caller-controlled session management at a higher recursion is outside scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no distinct complementary operational truth-checking actor returns independently judged findings into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: adapter output parsing, session identity verification and error reporting ensure transport correctness, not independent audit of agent claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: native agent reports its result; caller may commission an external reviewer but Harness has no dedicated autonomous audit decision. - Decision owner: none for complementary audit in shipped run; deterministic validators own only contract checking. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: output parsers, session telemetry, strict resume identity, tests and error types. - Closure path: parse/identity errors close or reject local transport; no alternative evidence channel feeds an independent corrective judgment back into work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adapter parsing and tests of Harness itself cannot substitute for a separate independently judging auditor of task execution. - Evidence: result parsing, session identity, supported integrations. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for standard integration. - Caveats: external test agents or independent reviewers can be composed outside the library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no system-level future/environment adaptation loop feeds developed options back into current operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new agents and SDK integrations enter through maintainer development, while task changes enter the native agent&#39;s S1 loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: developer/caller chooses installed adapters and integration capabilities; no in-boundary prospective adaptation authority is exposed. - Decision owner: external developers or caller rather than a first-party S4 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adapter capability tables, explicit backend options, runtime process telemetry and proposed adapter wishlist. - Closure path: upgraded library code can alter future runs through a software release, but no first-party autonomous prospective sensing → options → current-system regulatory return loop is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: configuration and extension maintenance is outside the one-session harness operating boundary; task planning inside the native agent is S1, not S4. - Evidence: adapter registry, deferred integration list, supported modes. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a future system built with Harness may create S4 externally.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-identity policy issue/decision/return loop is established for one caller-configured run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sandbox/permission choices and API credentials are run-level constraints, not charter disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller chooses permission policy and model/tool backend; native backend may prompt for tool approval; deterministic Harness validates allowed combinations. - Decision owner: caller and upstream native CLI for their respective configuration and approval decisions, neither shown as a shipped S5 ultimate-policy authority at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission bypass mapping, backend validation and instruction projection. - Closure path: static run options affect subprocess flags or reject invalid combinations; no identity-policy matter reaches legitimate ultimate authority and returns as governing system policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approving a tool call or selecting permissionpolicy does not decide organizational identity/ultimate purpose. - Evidence: RunSpec permission policy, validation and rejection, README policy boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: developer governance and host policy are adjacent, not automatically the operating system&#39;s S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e4a962f972504fc9e17ee3225e128c7936e1daa4/assessments/tw/twaldin-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e4a962f972504fc9e17ee3225e128c7936e1daa4\">e4a962f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:24:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hsaghir/looplet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Looplet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hsaghir/looplet/tree/7a7e1a296cb5d1d61a3cd4fcfc9eafbecab9a4d2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7a7e1a296cb5d1d61a3cd4fcfc9eafbecab9a4d2\">7a7e1a2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Task-directed model/tool work with local response to observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool errors, changing results, incomplete answers and context pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select next tool/action or propose completion in light of the updated conversation. - Decision owner: The configured model-driven task agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party parsing, tool registry, hooks, retention, compaction, limits and checkpoints. - Closure path: Dispatched results and rejected completion feedback enter subsequent model turns; accepted completion yields a result. - Boundary reachability: Public composableloop and async counterpart execute this path with configured backend and Python tools; no external agent harness owns the loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model discretion leaves dispatch/limits but no equivalent task-dependent action choice. - Evidence: loop.py, especially model-result handling and terminal-gate feedback; README.md; operations.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for source ownership; no provider run was executed here. - Caveats: Outcome correctness and safety depend on supplied tools, model and host isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuation of a specific disturbance among distinct S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The candidate regulation was checked at the declared runtime recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied function-specific discretionary closure was established. - Decision owner: No first-party owner for this function; caller configuration remains caller-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime hooks, persistence and limits support execution without acquiring organizational discretion. - Closure path: No material shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An autonomous S1 loop does not transfer ownership of this separate function. - Evidence: subagent.py; operations.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to this distribution. - Caveats: A downstream application can add its own organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-system current regulation of resources, commitments and constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The candidate regulation was checked at the declared runtime recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied function-specific discretionary closure was established. - Decision owner: No first-party owner for this function; caller configuration remains caller-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime hooks, persistence and limits support execution without acquiring organizational discretion. - Closure path: No material shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An autonomous S1 loop does not transfer ownership of this separate function. - Evidence: loop.py; subagent.py; operations.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to this distribution. - Caveats: A downstream application can add its own organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Complementary outcome-grounded challenge to a run&#39;s completion/correctness claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Accepted done() or plausible trajectory despite wrong external artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge outcome evidence separately from the producing agent, then use the finding to accept or correct operation. - Decision owner: Host-composed evaluator/auditor; autonomous judgment and corrective authority are not installed by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvalContext, collectors, grader-only expected data, EvalHook results, saved evidence, EvalReport/assertpassed and optional judge backend. - Closure path: The API exposes findings and failed required grading to the caller; host must connect them to corrective control. This incomplete composition supports C, not A. - Boundary reachability: EvalHook can attach directly to an ordinary public loop, run independent collectors after completion and expose assessment/results. It is an explicit outcome-evaluation constructor, not merely a generic callback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Framework collection/aggregation is not an independent audit judgment; protection and returned control require host composition. - Claim being audited: Completion/correctness of the task&#39;s final output. - Ordinary reporting path: Agent terminal output and run trajectory. - Complementary access path: Collectors inspect actual files, tests or other outcome artifacts and expose them to graders outside the agent-visible task. - Independence boundary: Separate collector/grader roles and grader-only expectations support complementary access; the host must preserve isolation and select independent judgment. - Who acts on findings: Host/auditor through returned EvalReport/results; no default corrective manager. - Evidence: evals.py; operations.md coding-test-freshness and protected-grading distinction; hooks.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the constructor, not a claim of a deployed independent audit. - Caveats: Routine completion hooks and self-controlled tests alone would not establish S3. EvalHook does not itself guarantee protected execution or correction.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/prospective intelligence returning adaptation options into current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The candidate regulation was checked at the declared runtime recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied function-specific discretionary closure was established. - Decision owner: No first-party owner for this function; caller configuration remains caller-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime hooks, persistence and limits support execution without acquiring organizational discretion. - Closure path: No material shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An autonomous S1 loop does not transfer ownership of this separate function. - Evidence: README.md; loop.py; operations.md; evals.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to this distribution. - Caveats: A downstream application can add its own organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity and ultimate-policy closure at the application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The candidate regulation was checked at the declared runtime recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied function-specific discretionary closure was established. - Decision owner: No first-party owner for this function; caller configuration remains caller-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime hooks, persistence and limits support execution without acquiring organizational discretion. - Closure path: No material shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An autonomous S1 loop does not transfer ownership of this separate function. - Evidence: README.md; operations.md; hooks.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to this distribution. - Caveats: A downstream application can add its own organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/38f4cbbbbd52c26a3b37d3f20bc4697afb0e8941/assessments/lo/looplet.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 38f4cbbbbd52c26a3b37d3f20bc4697afb0e8941\">38f4cbb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T18:45:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/patriceckhart/zot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/patriceckhart/zot/tree/2a19df15ba8278dd6a92240ab6c2ae267c3cf6c3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2a19df15ba8278dd6a92240ab6c2ae267c3cf6c3\">2a19df1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out open-ended coding and inspection by choosing local file/tool/shell actions with model-to-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repository state, edit/test consequences, command failures, multiple file/tool observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool and coding action based on the current messages and results. - Decision owner: first-party Zot core.Agent model-directed actor; external model endpoint supplies inference only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent model request, registered tools, native tool executor, first-party session state and credentials/provider adapters. - Closure path: task→oneTurn model tool calls→executeTools results appended to transcript→next runLoop iteration or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: core.Agent.runLoop is the shipped binary&#39;s ordinary interaction loop, and optional swarm child processes invoke their own first-party Zot agent loop, not an imported coding-agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: meaningful next task actions are model-directed rather than a fixed scripted series; runtime and human constraints bound these choices. - Evidence: packages/core/agent.go, packages/agent/swarmagent.go, packages/agent/modes/print.go. - Basis: explicit native source code. - Confidence: high for first-party path and feedback; no live model execution performed. - Caveats: performance and tool grants depend on model provider and operator configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination relation that diagnoses and attenuates concrete interference among separate swarm S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: swarm children share a writable workspace and may collide on edits; inter-worker write interference is a plausible disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate competing writes/task claims, return a collision finding or negotiated rescope to affected workers. No such native relation is established. - Decision owner: swarm management spawns independent child loops; parent can manually message children but no native inter-worker write-claim controller is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status/inbox and shared workspace; these are generic routing/status, not a conflict lock/arbiter. - Closure path: child completion returns a summary to parent, not a conflict-specific arbitration-and-correction cycle among worker S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent parallel work and communication do not establish S2 without a returned response to specific inter-S1 disturbance. README explicitly cautions that children edit the same files without isolation. - Evidence: README.md, packages/agent/swarm/swarm.go, packages/agent/modes/interactive.go, packages/agent/tools/swarmspawn.go. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high, at default shared-workspace swarm boundary. - Caveats: user-created external Git worktrees might attenuate conflicts but cannot be credited as shipped first-party automatic S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent-operator whole-swarm current supervision of worker commitments and allocation while a coding workspace is active. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale/failed/background child activity, task assignment and progress across multiple running Zot child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: view the swarm worker population and make actual current commitments by spawning another worker, redirecting work with a message, canceling/terminating work, or resuming a stopped worker. - Decision owner: human terminal operator through /swarm dashboard; the coding model&#39;s optional spawn tool is ordinary task delegation and does not prove a distinct autonomous S3 supervisor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: swarm SnapshotAll/current status, per-agent subprocess and inbox, session-scoped dashboard, SpawnReq, Stop, SendInput, Resume and child persistent state. - Closure path: whole-swarm dashboard status/activity view → operator&#39;s current worker assignment/intervention → first-party process spawn/send/stop/resume → live child run/inbox changes → updated statuses and outputs. - Boundary reachability: installed /swarm UI is a shipped optional interactive feature controlling first-party child Zot processes, rather than an external management application or developer-only test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decisive group-level current control belongs to the parent operator, hence P; auto-swarm alone is insufficient for autonomous S3. - Evidence: README.md, packages/agent/swarm/swarm.go, packages/agent/modes/swarmdialog.go, packages/agent/modes/interactive.go. - Whole-system current view: session-scoped swarm dashboard shows each child state, recent activity, error, task and lifecycle. - Current-control decision scope: operator changes which subordinate agents are running/paused, their task messages and active commitments from that group view. - Basis: first-party parent current supervision path. - Confidence: moderate for explicitly enabled operator swarm mode. - Caveats: S3 is not credited merely to auto-swarm summary or one ordinary coding session; no S3=A/C independent actor mode is established.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently owned complementary audit/verification function with a separate findings return and corrective disposition is shipped at this session/swarm boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous outputs, incomplete work or unverified code changes may occur, but review ability through ordinary model tools or optional user-installed skills is not S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect S1 claims/evidence and return findings into accountable current control; such a first-party distinct review path was not found. - Decision owner: one main coding model or manually spawned subagents under operator direction; no designated native auditor role/verdict authority mandatory for a specified supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic tool results, session logs, statuses and auto-swarm summary, without binding independent claim audit. - Closure path: generic child task report→parent summary only. No first-party independent audit claim→source access→finding→correction/stop gate beyond ordinary work completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: built-in children and a user-created reviewer prompt are generic S1/parent delegation, and optional extensions are not first-party audit owners. - Evidence: packages/core/agent.go, packages/agent/swarm/swarm.go, packages/agent/modes/interactive.go, packages/agent/tools/swarmspawn.go. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate, at inspected first-party standard distribution. - Caveats: user may spawn reviewer-like child tasks, but this is an external constructed audit; optional third-party extensions not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future/environment monitoring and owned prospective capability change loop at installed session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context pressure and recurring user tasks are handled by compaction/persistent instructions; not a distinct adaptive external-intelligence remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a future-facing change to the system&#39;s capability from environmental evidence and return it to present operation; absent. - Decision owner: operator can modify settings/skills, and main model can re-plan current tasks; neither qualifies without outside-and-then strategy closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session compaction, per-project AGENTS.md, skills, extension loader, model selection, persistence. - Closure path: ongoing task context restored or compacted; no evidence-first adaptation option→binding prospective system change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, model switching and user skill installation are configuration/continuity mechanisms rather than S4 intelligence adaptation. - Evidence: packages/core/compact.go, packages/agent/skillpins.go, packages/agent/extcmd.go, packages/agent/settingsstore.go. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external operator workflows can evolve installed Zot, outside credited first-party S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate organizational identity/ultimate-policy decision loop at this local coding/swam boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool approvals and provider credentials protect local execution, without adjudicating ultimate organizational purpose or policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue a binding ultimate identity/policy interpretation and return it to current/swarm operations; not evidenced. - Decision owner: human configures permissions, credentials and agent identity; runtime applies configured access bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent options, user tool confirmation, jail settings and extension policies. - Closure path: permission allows/denies a tool and config changes future tool availability, but neither establishes first-party rightful S5 identity authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent S3 operational control over child sessions does not automatically amount to ultimate organizational policy and purpose adjudication. - Evidence: packages/agent/config.go, packages/core/confirm.go, packages/agent/cli.go. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external user or organization may provide a parent S5, not credited at this installed binary recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d9c402e6ce327f8e8c3afaa6837bcbd50b3e832/assessments/zot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d9c402e6ce327f8e8c3afaa6837bcbd50b3e832\">2d9c402 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T20:46:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/folke/zaly\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">zaly</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/folke/zaly/tree/a568c033de6418e5a24193f41ae8335ce0ddb2b2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a568c033de6418e5a24193f41ae8335ce0ddb2b2\">a568c03 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform a user coding request through model-selected inspection, edit, shell and related first-party tools, adapting steps to returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository organization, user implementation requirements, tool failures, slow process output and session context limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured language model selects task-specific tool calls and continuation after returned tool results, while permission checks may reject certain actions. - Decision owner: First-party Agent per-session model loop with configured provider; Tasks/tool handlers carry out those decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Streaming model adapter, default bash/edit/read/write/fetch/find/grep tools, tool result/session injection, background task poll/heartbeat, session JSONL replay, compaction/masking and permissions. - Closure path: User input → model tool selection → typed tool dispatch → execution result or permission denial → next model step, explicit stop or later task completion injected into session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses specific coding interventions and follow-up actions; fixed tools, prompts and permission presets constrain but do not replace that operative decision. - Boundary reachability: Default CLI loads createAgent and sets tools.active from user/project config defaults; its selected tool handlers execute through the same agent&#39;s step loop. - Evidence: standard CLI agent construction, model stream and step, run continuation, background results. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for first-party model/action wiring; no empirical live completion claim. - Caveats: Tool safety and task success depend on selected permission preset, provider availability and actual host behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Dampen real interference among distinct independently viable operational S1 cells and return coordination adjustments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential file/edit collisions if multiple coding models execute in the same workspace; simultaneous tool calls and task result ordering in one coding loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The default CLI excludes both named swarm agent-spawn/message tools and one-shot subagent; no installed inter-S1 conflict arbitration is reachable in that mode. - Decision owner: None established for a qualifying S2. Tasks sequences parallel/nonparallel tool calls and the optional Swarm transports messages when separately activated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-tool parallel flags, task chain ordering, user permissions, freshness checks, async completion injection and unselected SDK child/swarm APIs. - Closure path: Concurrent tools → deterministic task queuing or result notification → same S1 model; no inter-S1 conflict → coordination decision → independently adjusted S1 behavior closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, lifecycle and tool sequencing are not a dedicated disturbance-regulation function among operating cells; disabled subagent tools cannot be credited as shipped CLI inter-agent control. - Evidence: default tools explicitly omit agents, CLI loads active defaults, task call sequencing, unselected swarm implementation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on default tool exposure, medium-high for bounded S2 classification. - Caveats: An opt-in consumer of the SDK may create operating subagents; that other supported mode needs independent evidence of concrete cross-agent interference and adjustment before grading S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Control current whole-system commitments, resources and priorities across operational divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task queue congestion, simultaneous tool usage, session/context budget and pending work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independent whole-current resource/commitment controller is evidenced; the parent coding model chooses individual tasks and users choose model/configuration settings. - Decision owner: No S3-specific owner in the default single-agent CLI. Tasks implements deterministic per-session pending/run/completion statuses. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background tasks, task list/poll/stop, session statuses, token accounting, compaction and permission limits. - Closure path: Task submitted → tool registry executes/queues → result enters one model&#39;s context; not a managerial decision over independently operating S1 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task scheduler mechanics and user controls cannot substitute for whole-current cross-cell decision authority. - Evidence: default single root, task manager, CLI user model selection. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The SDK can be embedded in a larger manager; no such separate current-control loop is credited at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently verify current-management operating claims against alternative operational reality and return corrective accountability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading task completion, unsafe edits, stale file assumptions and inaccurate run history. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party independent auditor of S3&#39;s current-management claim is wired; source tests, permissions and source-read freshness are task-local correctness/support. - Decision owner: None for S3; human project reviewers and test suites outside the running agent are adjacent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool result logs, session/message tree, edit freshness checks, activity status and tests in the developer repository. - Closure path: Tool/file observation → same operating model or human approval; no S3 → independent alternative reality audit → corrected S3 decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Inspecting output and enforcing write preconditions increases task reliability but does not create complementary management-audit authority. - Evidence: message/session state, file edit freshness and preflight, default tools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An external human or plugin could perform review but is not a packaged S3 operating channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Observe and model prospective external change, choose adaptation strategies and return new capability commitments through current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project requirements, model-provider capabilities and future workflows could matter to the coding tool. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user may change provider/models/tools/resources; the runtime masks or compacts local history. No installed autonomous prospective external-environment options/adoption decision is shown. - Decision owner: Human and configuration/resource manager for capability selection, not a first-party S4 strategist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider registry and model switch, skills/plugins, resource pack reload, local token/compaction/masking and user commands. - Closure path: Current context pressure or user settings → changed local prompt/resources. No external future → evaluated strategic alternative → binding S3 operating return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hackable plugins, resource loaders and provider choice give extensibility, not agent-owned proactive adaptation to a forecast external environment. - Evidence: resource and model customization, CLI resource load/config, context masking/compaction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A custom skill could research future change, but that user-installed or task-instructed extension is a different candidate path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately settle ultimate identity and governing values for the coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous shell commands, editing without authorization, untrusted project instructions and conflicting user preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human chooses requested work, tool permissions and default presets. The runtime applies allow/ask/deny and user prompts; it has no legitimate independent final-purpose/policy decision. - Decision owner: Human operator/project configuration, mediated by deterministic PermissionManager and tool-specific gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission presets strict, readonly, permissive, yolo, per-tool allow/ask/deny rules and user approval callback. - Closure path: User/host policy → tool execution preflight → allow, ask or deny local coding action. It is enforcement, not agent-originated ultimate governance with return to S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Having a default prompt that calls the agent adaptive or a rule engine controlling tools does not authorize redefinition of the organization&#39;s identity and values. - Evidence: CLI permissions configuration, PermissionManager and presets, operator warning. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high about preset/human authority; medium-high about S5 absence. - Caveats: The explicitly permissive yolo mode changes protections, not VSM S5 decision ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a2fbb89ca6542b43c9b27861ad875883cd566ea0/assessments/za/zaly.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a2fbb89ca6542b43c9b27861ad875883cd566ea0\">a2fbb89 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:20:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Browser Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness/tree/afbcc381b963040c19627d788e40c7e7663171ee\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision afbcc381b963040c19627d788e40c7e7663171ee\">afbcc38 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: autonomous interactive browser task execution in the live web environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: page state, navigation, DOM/accessibility structure, authentication/session state, dynamic UI and task-specific browser mechanics. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven agent chooses browser actions, inspections, retries and task-specific helper additions. - Decision owner: autonomous agent actor operating through the first-party skill/control surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CDP helper layer, daemon, tab state, MCP/CLI transport, diagnostics/recovery and editable helper workspace. - Closure path: browser actions change page/environment state; subsequent observations and agent decisions consume those results until the task outcome is reached. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C. - Distinct S1 units: multiple agent actors may concurrently perform browser work through the same local Chrome/harness boundary. - Specific interference: one default daemon has one mutable attached/current tab; simultaneous tab switching/actions can race, causing an agent to act on or capture another agent&#39;s tab. - Coordination relation: the shipped skill requires browser operations through the default local daemon to be serialized; for true simultaneity it directs each task to an isolated remote browser, with a named local daemon only as a last-resort isolation mechanism. - Closure path: the chosen serialization/isolation arrangement changes later S1 browser behaviour by preventing concurrent mutation of the shared lane or separating the lanes entirely. - Ownership: Browser Harness supplies the S2-specific disturbance model and regulation protocol, but it does not itself establish a single autonomous first-party coordinator that chooses ordering/isolation across arbitrary external hosts. The path therefore requires composition with the host/orchestrator/participating agents. - Why not generic routing: the evidence identifies a concrete mutable-tab collision and a regulation response designed specifically to attenuate it. - Basis /\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The daemon manages connection/tab state and remote-browser lifecycle, but no distinct actor is established with a whole-system current view and discretionary authority over organization-wide resources, priorities or commitments. Connection recovery and lifecycle enforcement are supporting runtime mechanisms rather than S3 ownership. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - Recordings, screenshots, diagnostics and verification steps improve observability and task execution but do not form an independent complementary audit channel with separate judgment and corrective return into current control. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Agent-written reusable helpers can improve future task capability, but they arise from current task mechanics and are generated by the operating agent. The pinned distribution does not establish an externally/prospectively oriented environmental-intelligence loop that develops adaptation options and closes them into present capability through S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Skill rules, browser permission prompts and user choices such as recording/cloud usage constrain operation but do not establish an identity/ultimate-policy decision loop at the harness recursion. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029/assessments/browser-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029\">96a7716 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T14:57:00Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/surefire-ai/korus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Korus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/surefire-ai/korus/tree/ed5ea6a9fca86f43df161a100e913c29fa9b40c9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed5ea6a9fca86f43df161a100e913c29fa9b40c9\">ed5ea6a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform declared agent work through first-party autonomous model/tool execution loops and return operational outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task uncertainty, model responses, tool/retrieval observations, intermediate reasoning, SubAgent results and stopping decisions needed to satisfy the AgentRun input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning steps, permitted tool calls or routing actions, interpret observations and decide when an operational answer is complete. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor executed through the first-party Korus Eino pattern runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled artifact, prompt/model/tool/knowledge bindings, tool allowlists, iteration limits, worker runtime, gateway, AgentRun lifecycle and trace/artifact persistence. - Closure path: AgentRun reaches the Korus worker → first-party pattern runner invokes the model → model selects a permitted action/tool or final response → tool/SubAgent/model result feeds back into the first-party loop → the loop continues or returns the operational result → AgentRun records the outcome. - Boundary reachability: EinoADKRunner and its built-in pattern handlers are the shipped production worker path at the pinned revision; the release/readiness history explicitly notes that later main replaced the old placeholder with the real Eino runtime and records a passed ReAct smoke test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Korus supplies the loop, context, permitted action surface and feedback transport; the model actor owns the substantive task-local action choice. External model-provider implementation is an environmental dependency rather than a separate agent harness that owns the organizational loop. - Evidence: internal/worker/einorunner.go, internal/worker/reactrunner.go, docs/phase2/agent-patterns-and-a2a-todo.md, docs/releases/v0.1.0-readiness.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic workflow mode and direct single-model fallback are additional supported modes; the A finding is grounded in the shipped autonomous ReAct/router/reflection/plan-execute/tool-calling paths, not in deterministic graph sequencing alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Korus can route work to SubAgents, execute DAG dependencies and pass parent state into nested AgentRuns, but no inspected standard path identifies a concrete interaction-generated conflict, inconsistency or oscillation among distinct S1 units and attenuates that disturbance through an S2-specific relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: router classification, SubAgent references/invocation, workflow graph edges, state propagation and compiler cycle checks. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a router chooses which specialist receives work and a workflow sequences nodes, but delegation/routing/sequencing are not S2 unless tied to a reconstructed inter-S1 disturbance and returned coordination feedback. - Evidence: internal/worker/routerrunner.go, internal/worker/workflowrunner.go, docs/phase2/agent-patterns-and-a2a-todo.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: full Agent Mesh/A2A is explicitly future work at this revision; downstream compositions may introduce S2-specific regulation without changing this repository-relative result.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or constructor whole-system current-control function is established at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the operator tracks readiness and current run state and enforces configured cancellation, deadlines, retry limits, workspace/provider constraints and lifecycle phase, but these are deterministic reconciliations of already-authored rules/requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: discretionary choices such as desired lifecycle phase, cancellation and retry/deadline parameters remain external spec/operator decisions; the controllers enforce them. - Decision owner: no first-party autonomous S3 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent and AgentRun status, Kubernetes reconcilers, workspace defaults, compiler validation, cancellation, timeout, retry/backoff and runtime status transitions. - Closure path: external specification/current request → deterministic controller reconciliation/enforcement → Agent/AgentRun state changes. No autonomous whole-system current view → discretionary commitment/resource/intervention decision → returned operational change loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: controller authority to retry, fail, cancel or publish according to authored fields is enforcement authority, not evidence that an autonomous actor owns the organizational current-control choice. - Evidence: internal/controller/agentcontroller.go, internal/controller/agentruncontroller.go, api/v1alpha1/agenttypes.go. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the Web Console and operator give humans substantial operational control, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award S3 parent notation from generic configuration/lifecycle intervention without an evidenced whole-system parent current-control loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently exercise a target Agent revision against a declared evaluation dataset, optionally compare a separate baseline, derive evaluator/threshold results and expose a gate verdict/report as complementary evidence about operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that ordinary successful AgentRun reporting accurately implies the target revision satisfies externally declared quality/behaviour expectations across representative samples or against a baseline. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party AgentEvaluation contract exposes an audit-specific GatePassed result derived from evaluator/threshold outcomes and baseline comparison; downstream composition must still decide how that verdict changes release/current operation. - Decision owner: no autonomous audit authority is bundled for the final corrective decision. The first-party evaluation controller owns execution and deterministic derivation of the declared audit contract, while the developer/operator must compose the actor/authority and return closure that consumes the verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentEvaluation CRD, Dataset samples/expected values, evaluator and threshold specs, optional baseline Agent/revision, controller-owned evaluation AgentRuns, metric aggregation, report construction and GatePassed status. - Closure path: AgentEvaluation selects target revision + independent dataset/evaluator contract → evaluation controller creates separate managed AgentRuns → results are evaluated/thresholded and optionally compared with a baseline → GatePassed and report are returned on AgentEvaluation status → constructor gap: no standard release/current-control actor consumes that verdict to alter subsequent operation at this revision. - Boundary reachability: AgentEvaluation is a shipped first-party CRD/controller in the standard operator, not repository-only benchmark/test code. The Phase 3 audit confirms evaluation foundations exist while separately identifying the missing release-gate decision/waiver/approval closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Korus intentionally exposes the complementary audit path itself, which is enough for C; it does not ship an autonomous actor that owns the decisive audit-to-operation response, so the path is not A. - Evidence: api/v1alpha1/supportingresourcetypes.go, internal/controller/agentevaluationcontroller.go, docs/phase3/phase3-completeness-audit.md, internal/controller/agentcontroller.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the audit contract can be human-authored and much evaluator logic is deterministic. C is based on the first-party S3-specific feedback construction path, not on treating deterministic threshold enforcement as autonomous organizational judgment. A future release-gate/current-control consumer could change the ownership classification on reassessment. - Claim being audited: the selected current Agent revision satisfies the declared dataset/evaluator/threshold contract and, when configured, compares acceptably with the selected baseline. - Ordinary reporting path: ordinary AgentRuns expose run phase, output, trace references, artifacts and agent revision through the normal execution lifecycle. - Complementary access path: the AgentEvaluation controller independently creates its own labelled managed AgentRuns over Dataset samples and evaluates those outputs against expected values/evaluators/thresholds, with optional separate baseline runs. - Independence boundary: evaluation has a distinct CRD/controller, sample set, evaluator/threshold contract and managed-run ownership from ordinary invocation/reporting. It exercises the same target Agent/runtime intentionally, but does not rely solely on the ordinary AgentRun&#39;s claim of success. - Who acts on findings: no standard first-party release/current-control actor at the reviewed revision; the developer/operator must compose a consumer of GatePassed/reporting, which is the reason for C rather than A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reflection can improve the output of the current task and evaluation can identify regressions/threshold failures, but neither path autonomously converts future/environment distinctions into persistent capability changes for later operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor owns a prospective adaptation choice over the organization&#39;s persistent capability/repertoire. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reflection generate/critique/revise loop, evaluation baseline comparison, Agent/Skill/Prompt/Policy declarative resources, revisioned compilation and roadmap/product-development feedback. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding; no outside/future distinction → adaptation option → returned persistent capability change loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reflection is task-local S1 self-correction and evaluation produces audit evidence. Updating prompts, agents, skills, policies or releases from that evidence remains an external developer/operator action at this revision. - Evidence: internal/worker/reflectionrunner.go, internal/controller/agentevaluationcontroller.go, docs/phase3/phase3-completeness-audit.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future release/revision workflows may connect evaluation evidence to adaptation, but design intent and developer iteration are not credited as runtime S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AgentPolicy, workspace provider policy, guardrails, budgets, allowed models/tools and desired lifecycle phase constrain what the system may do, but they are authored externally and deterministically resolved/enforced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: identity, ultimate purpose and top-level policy contents remain with the user/operator/developer outside the first-party autonomous runtime. - Decision owner: no first-party autonomous or operationally closed parent S5 authority established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPolicy CRD, workspace policy/defaults, Agent desired phase, prompt/spec resources, compiler validation and runtime/tool constraints. - Closure path: external authority authors policy/specification → first-party compiler/controller/runtime enforce it. No identity/ultimate-policy issue is autonomously adjudicated and returned through a first-party S5 closure loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static policy objects and hard enforcement express governance constraints but do not themselves own the ultimate policy decision right. - Evidence: api/v1alpha1/supportingresourcetypes.go, api/v1alpha1/agenttypes.go, internal/controller/agentcontroller.go. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human/operator authorship is real governance, but generic configuration authority does not establish Methodology P without a reconstructed identity/ultimate-policy issue and return-to-operation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cf0d144ae67e55ca5541320f7119a0ccf6a7926f/assessments/korus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cf0d144ae67e55ca5541320f7119a0ccf6a7926f\">cf0d144 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T22:58:51Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codejunkie99/agentic-stack\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agentic-stack</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codejunkie99/agentic-stack/tree/8fd5726aaebbd421b835ad1c71f2c66064b74caf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8fd5726aaebbd421b835ad1c71f2c66064b74caf\">8fd5726 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended project/task work using the installed portable context, skills and protocols, observe task/environment results, and return an operational result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task ambiguity, project/repository state, tool/model feedback, remembered conventions, skill selection, failures and other task-local distinctions that require non-preenumerated action choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete reasoning/action response to the current task within the supplied context and constraints and revise that response from model/tool/project feedback. - Decision owner: the autonomous model-backed agent actor reached through the shipped standalone Python/conductor mode; external compatible harness agents can occupy the same operational role but are not needed to establish the first-party standalone path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context-budget builder; portable memory/skills/protocol files; configured provider selection; execution logging; permissions/hooks; optional recall/search; and adapter/install machinery. - Closure path: standalone-python/run.py receives a task → conductor.run builds the first-party system context → llm.callmodel invokes the configured model actor → the actor makes the task-level response decision → conductor logs success/failure and returns the result to the operating session. - Boundary reachability: the repository ships adapters/standalone-python/run.py, .agent/harness/conductor.py, and .agent/harness/llm.py as a supported adapter path. It therefore reaches an autonomous model actor without requiring the organizational internals of Claude Code/Codex/other adjacent harnesses. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while leaving conductor, context assembly, local files and logging intact leaves no mechanism that makes the open-ended task decision. The deterministic harness supports and records S1; the agent owns the operative discretion. - Evidence: README.md; adapters/standalone-python/run.py; .agent/harness/conductor.py; .agent/harness/llm.py; docs/architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the provider service and model implementation are external infrastructure. This finding credits the shipped first-party adapter/control path that places the model actor in the operational role; it does not inherit provider-side organizational functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate workspace/branch interference among distinct mutating bounded-loop S1 runs by assigning each run an owned isolated Git worktree and retaining that isolation across retries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent or overlapping maker runs against one project could collide in the active checkout, overwrite one another&#39;s working changes, or make changed-path verification ambiguous. - Distinct S1 units: separate bounded loop runs, each with its own maker agent process and task-local operational work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-workspace mutation would couple otherwise independent runs through the same checkout/files; the design explicitly states that separate runs never share a worktree and action-capable loops require isolation because reliable per-run change attribution matters. - Attenuating coordination relation: createworktree() allocates a unique run-id-specific branch/worktree from the validated repository base; the runner executes that run&#39;s maker and verifier in the owned path; other runs receive different paths. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordination result is not merely recorded metadata: the owned worktree becomes executionroot, so all later maker retries, verifier checks and changed-path policy for that run occur inside the isolated workspace rather than the shared active checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path is specifically designed to prevent cross-run mutation interference and preserve independent run identity/forensics. It is not credited from generic delegation, a queue, or the shared memory layer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and bind a run to a unique owned worktree/branch so mutating S1 cells do not operate on the same checkout. - Decision owner: no autonomous coordination owner is shipped for this right. The first-party loop runtime deterministically applies the isolation contract; the operator/developer supplies the maker profiles and initiates the runs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run-id generation; repository/common-dir identity verification; safe worktree root checks; unique branch naming; checkpointed ownership metadata; changed-path collection; refusal-safe cleanup. - Closure path: a mutating loop is started → runner creates/verifies a unique worktree → that path becomes the maker&#39;s execution root → retries/verifier/checker remain bound to the same run worktree → independent runs receive separate worktrees, attenuating shared-checkout interference in subsequent S1 action. - Boundary reachability: the bounded-loop commands, worktree implementation, default L2/L3 worktree contract and checkpoint machinery are shipped first-party and are installed by loop init; a user supplies compatible maker commands but does not have to invent the isolation/feedback path itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing any autonomous coordinating agent leaves materially the same unique-worktree decision and enforcement. Conversely, the shipped default loop role profiles are placeholders rather than a first-party autonomous coordination actor. The function-specific S2 path is therefore constructor-level C, not A. - Evidence: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-agentic-loops-meta-harness-design.md; harnessmanager/loops/runner.py; harnessmanager/loops/worktrees.py; .agent/loops/harnesses.json. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this S2 witness is specifically the bounded-loop multi-run mode, not the mere fact that many adapter types can share .agent/. The current release does not supply an autonomous agent that decides cross-run coordination policy, so the state is intentionally limited to C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control loop is established across the full assessed agentic-stack operational population. - Disturbance / variety regulated: individual loop budgets, attempts, policy violations, cancellation and approval are regulated; local dashboards summarize activity across harnesses; neither surface closes whole-system resource/commitment/prioritization control over all current S1 cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor inside the credited boundary is shown deciding current priorities, resource allocation, commitments, synergy or intervention for the whole heterogeneous set of agent sessions represented by the installed stack. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared system boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-loop attempt/runtime/token/output breakers; per-run cancellation; project-wide bounded-loop pause-all; loop status; local data-layer dashboards and Mission Control read projections; operator approvals. - Closure path: no whole-system S3-specific decision-and-return path spans the complete assessed operational population. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: pause-all is a strong operator intervention but it only governs agentic-stack bounded-loop runners, while Mission Control/data-layer visibility also represents external adapter sessions and cron work that the pause path does not control. Per-loop breakers enforce preselected constraints rather than own whole-system managerial discretion. - Evidence: docs/data-layer.md; harnessmanager/loops/commands.py; harnessmanager/loops/runner.py; harnessmanager/missioncontrolserver.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a narrower recursion containing only bounded loop runs could expose operator stop/status control, but this assessment fixes the shipped installed agentic-stack project boundary rather than silently shrinking the system to make a positive S3 witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge a maker&#39;s claim that a bounded software task is complete after deterministic verification, then return approve/reject/escalate findings into retry or completion control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, semantically inadequate changes that nevertheless pass deterministic tests, repeated flawed approaches, and cases requiring human judgment. - Claim being audited: the maker&#39;s current attempt satisfies the original task and constraints after the deterministic verifier has passed. - Ordinary reporting path: maker process result, changed-file list, deterministic verifier stdout/stderr/exit status and checkpoint/event history. - Complementary access path: a separately named checker profile is launched in a fresh process after verifier success with review instructions and access to the same isolated project state; its structured verdict is parsed separately from the maker result. - Independence boundary: the first-party protocol requires checker profile name separation and a fresh process, but permits the same underlying binary and ships only placeholder custom-agent profiles. Sufficiently independent autonomous reviewer identity/access must therefore be composed by the user/operator. - Who acts on findings: runner.py deterministically maps APPROVE to completion, REJECT to feedback and another bounded maker attempt, and ESCALATE/malformed output to a paused run requiring human judgment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: maker self-confirmation and semantic defects not captured by deterministic verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue the complementary semantic audit verdict that determines whether the attempt completes, retries with feedback, or escalates. - Decision owner: constructor-level only in the standard distribution. A checker agent can own the judgment once a genuinely independent compatible checker profile is supplied; the bundled placeholder does not itself establish that autonomous owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic verifier gate; distinct checker profile key; fresh process invocation; structured verdict grammar; attempt budget; checkpoint/event log; worktree preservation for inspection. - Closure path: maker attempt → deterministic verifier pass → checker process reviews task/project outcome → APPROVE completes, REJECT becomes feedback in the next maker prompt, or ESCALATE pauses for human judgment → subsequent operation changes according to the finding. - Boundary reachability: checker invocation, result parsing, retry/escalation closure and the checker role contract are all shipped in the bounded-loop runtime. The missing piece is the independently controlled checker actor/profile, which is intentionally user-composed through the shipped profile schema. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit/feedback path exists, but the default checker command is a custom-agent placeholder and same-binary checker use is explicitly permitted. Agentic-stack therefore cannot claim that the standard distribution itself owns an independent autonomous audit judgment. - Evidence: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-agentic-loops-meta-harness-design.md; harnessmanager/loops/runner.py; .agent/loops/harnesses.json; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic verification alone is not credited as S3. The C state specifically reflects the first-party checker/audit decision path while preserving the unresolved independence/actor composition boundary.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party externally/prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established that models future environment distinctions, develops options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the system does learn from past execution, stage recurring patterns, recall memory, flag repeated failures and permit skill rewrites, but those are retrospective/internal learning paths rather than a demonstrated outside-and-then intelligence loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is shown owning a prospective environment/adaptation judgment satisfying the S4 witness at this boundary. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: episodic/semantic memory; staging-only dream cycle; Skillforge/self-rewrite hooks; failure counters; data flywheel exports; dashboards/KPIs; upgrade and skill-manifest machinery. - Closure path: no S4-specific external/future distinction → option generation → return-to-current-capability loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous agents may use memories or create/update skills during task work, but the reviewed standard distribution does not show a distinct prospective environmental intelligence function whose options are fed back to alter present organizational capability. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; .agent/memory/autodream.py; .agent/skills/skillforge/SKILL.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the repository contains future-facing design/spec material and an optional data flywheel, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat roadmap/design documents, retrospective learning, or future training possibilities as S4 without the operational prospective loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established for the installed agentic-stack organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, approval-required actions, deny paths, budgets and user preferences constrain operations, but they are static/operator-authored policies or operational approvals rather than S5 identity deliberation and closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or parent path is shown receiving an identity/ultimate-policy issue, deciding it as legitimate ultimate authority, and returning that decision to govern subsequent operation as an S5 loop. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: human-owned permissions.md; pre-tool-call enforcement; loop constraints/budgets; approval gates; personal preferences; installer configuration; user-controlled feature toggles. - Closure path: no qualifying S5 decision-and-return path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permissions.md explicitly says humans edit it and the agent may not modify/bypass it. That makes ultimate constraints externally configured, but static parent authorship and ordinary action approval do not by themselves establish parent-governed S5 under Methodology 0.3.6. - Evidence: .agent/protocols/permissions.md; docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-agentic-loops-meta-harness-design.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user clearly remains an ultimate authority outside the autonomous task loop and can edit policies, approve sensitive actions or stop work. Those ordinary operator powers are not published as S5=P without evidence of a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy matter and closed return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20b70cdc8b17b6a3c9568bf24a1d5eef2507e6d1/assessments/agentic-stack.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20b70cdc8b17b6a3c9568bf24a1d5eef2507e6d1\">20b70cd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:48:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Meta-Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stanford-iris-lab/meta-harness/tree/0cbc31e97c9e6d24232d1dc754827c02e1ec415c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0cbc31e97c9e6d24232d1dc754827c02e1ec415c\">0cbc31e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. The system&#39;s purpose is automated search over task-specific harnesses. Its supplied loop invokes a proposer agent, validates generated candidates, benchmarks them and iterates against the current frontier without requiring a human decision each iteration. That closed search trajectory is the operational transformation at this boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Candidate systems, proposer calls and benchmark jobs are stages or alternatives inside one search operation. The reviewed framework does not establish two or more operational S1 units plus a concrete mutual-interference regulation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. Frontier bookkeeping, iteration control and candidate validation govern the search procedure itself. They do not establish a distinct metasystem actor with whole-organization current view and authority over shared organizational resources, commitments or priorities.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Validation benchmarking is the normal production feedback used by the search. A held-out test path exists, but it is explicitly run only when the evolution run is finalized and frozen against further evolution; its findings therefore do not feed back into subsequent current control within that run. No separate corrective audit closure is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. The historical positive classified evolutionary harness optimization as S4. Under the function-first criterion, candidate generation and validation against task benchmarks are the primary operation of this meta-harness itself, and the information source is task-internal performance evidence. No separate outside-and-then environmental intelligence function relating future/external change back to present organizational capability was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Domain specifications, objectives, benchmark configuration and user initiation define the search problem, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529/assessments/meta-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529\">911ebc9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:26:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ScaleML/AgentSPEX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentSPEX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ScaleML/AgentSPEX/tree/db99b7b6802fe15ef2997c82ec1d49e5228128f6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db99b7b6802fe15ef2997c82ec1d49e5228128f6\">db99b7b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a user task/workflow objective into an answer or tool-mediated environmental result through model-driven reasoning/planning/action with returned execution feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, workspace/environment state, tool results/errors, model outputs, variable/context state, plan failures and bounded execution conditions encountered while completing one objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive model outputs, next task actions/tool calls, and in agentic-loop mode the decomposition/plan content used to pursue the current objective. - Decision owner: the configured autonomous LLM actor invoked by AgentSPEX&#39;s first-party execution/agentic-loop paths. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSPEX, LLM executor, workflow interpreter, tool registry/MCP integration, agentic-loop exploration/decomposition/planning, task board, plan verifier/executor, checkpoints, traces, limits and sandbox/runtime support. - Closure path: user goal/current context → first-party runtime calls the model → model selects output/tool action or task plan → runtime validates/executes permitted work → tool/environment/step results return into workflow/agent context → model revises/continues or synthesizes the final result. - Boundary reachability: the documented src/harness/run.py entry directly selects normal modes or loop mode and invokes the shipped runtime. No downstream orchestration package is needed beyond a configured model/provider and external tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining the deterministic interpreter, plan validator and tool executor removes the substantive task/decomposition/next-action judgment; those host mechanisms enforce or apply decisions rather than substitute the same judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/harness/run.py; src/harness/agent.py; src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference and external tools may be outside the repository, but the first-party distribution concretely closes their decisions/results inside the supported task execution loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation function is established at the assessed task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete interaction-generated conflict/oscillation between distinct credited sibling S1 units is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML parallel, gather, foreach, sub-workflows, task-board ordering, variable dependencies, isolated sandboxes and concurrency controls can schedule/combine work but do not establish S2 by themselves. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generated subtasks and branches are decomposition of one objective. The reviewed runtime does not identify peer-S1 interference and feed an attenuation decision back into those peer operations. - Evidence: docs/workflow-language.md; src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py; src/harness/agenticloop/taskboard.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream workflows can compose multiple agents, but generic composition/fan-out is not credited without a concrete S2 disturbance-and-attenuation witness owned by AgentSPEX.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control function is established above the single task/workflow organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current subtask state, step dependencies, retries, plan validity, token/tool limits, checkpoints and optional plan approval are regulated locally within one objective rather than through a higher whole-organization control loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct whole-system judgment over current resources, commitments, priorities, constraints or cross-S1 intervention is established at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task board, generated plan, deterministic interpreter/control flow, plan verifier, configuration limits, checkpoint/resume, user plan approval and execution lifecycle events. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Whole-system current view: TaskBoard exposes the current decomposition/status/results for one user objective, but that is the operating agent&#39;s own task state rather than a distinct metasystem view over multiple autonomous operational commitments. - Current-control decision scope: current plan/subtask sequencing and bounded execution of one task; no separate current resource/commitment authority at a higher recursion is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM decomposition/planning is part of S1 task pursuit. Deterministic status tracking, workflow scheduling and a human approval gate do not establish a separate S3 function or parent S3 mode. - Evidence: src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py; src/harness/agenticloop/taskboard.py; src/harness/agenticloop/planexecutor.py; src/harness/run.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the word “plan” or task-board visibility is not treated as S3 when it is internal decomposition/control of one S1 objective.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit loop is established at the standard runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: structural workflow defects and ordinary runtime errors are checked/recorded, but no independent path is shown auditing actual operating claims and returning complementary findings into later organizational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying independent audit judgment-and-return right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agentic-loop PlanVerifier, standalone Lean AgentVerifier, checkpoints/traces/replay, dashboard/structured events, benchmark evaluators and demo-specific reviewers/refiners. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard runtime path closes ordinary operational evidence → complementary independent access → audit finding → returned corrective/control decision. - Claim being audited: not established as a runtime organizational claim distinct from ordinary execution validity. - Ordinary reporting path: model/workflow outputs, tool results, task-board/status events and execution traces. - Complementary access path: no qualifying standard operational path established. PlanVerifier and Lean verification inspect plan/workflow structure rather than independently observing actual S1 behavior. - Independence boundary: not established for a runtime audit loop. Benchmark/demo evaluators may have separate criteria but are adjacent application/evaluation systems rather than a generic standard runtime feedback owner. - Who acts on findings: no qualifying complementary findings path is established; structural validation blocks malformed plans before execution and ordinary runtime errors remain on the normal control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: structural verification and telemetry can support correctness/observability but do not establish the S3 function without an independent observation/claim path and return closure. - Evidence: src/harness/agenticloop/verifier.py; verifier/README.md; src/harness/run.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the standalone Lean verifier can prove structural graph properties and a downstream benchmark can separately grade an AgentSPEX workflow, but neither is credited as a standard complementary operational feedback loop at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established that turns prior environmental evidence into a persistent changed AgentSPEX capability or control strategy for later operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task state, stored episodic information, checkpoints, trace data and within-task planning can influence continued work, but no distinct future-facing capability adaptation is generated and durably applied. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is established selecting a persistent future runtime/harness change from accumulated external evidence. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: episodic memory store/search/retrieve, checkpoints, resume, trace replay, workspace state, LLM plan revision and reusable YAML/submodule definitions. - Closure path: not applicable; no observed external evidence → prospective adaptation option → durable capability/strategy mutation → later operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory and recovery preserve information/current work; planner revisions pursue the current objective. They do not modify the future harness or generate a persistent adaptation option from environmental evidence. - Evidence: src/sandboxvm/tools/memory/episodic.py; src/harness/run.py; src/harness/agenticloop/loop.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistent episodic data can improve later context if a workflow chooses to retrieve it, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not equate durable memory/context reuse with S4 capability adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed first-party runtime loop is established for deciding or reaffirming AgentSPEX&#39;s ultimate identity, purpose or foundational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: YAML/system prompts, tool availability, model/configuration, runtime limits, sandbox constraints and optional user plan approval shape operation, but they are externally authored inputs/gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor receives an identity/ultimate-policy issue and returns an authoritative foundational decision governing subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow configuration, system prompts, tool/MCP registration, environment variables, sandbox/VM restrictions, plan approval and structural workflow validation. - Closure path: not applicable; no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM can plan/decompose work within a supplied objective and workflow/tool boundary, but no supported path lets it own the mandate itself. Human approval of one plan is a bounded action gate rather than a runtime S5 identity-policy loop. - Evidence: README.md; src/harness/run.py; docs/workflow-language.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong static rules/configuration and human gating can enforce policy but do not establish ownership of ultimate policy/identity under the Profile closure test.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6b3d8c0916f09f4f067aca760a92db3904ec93e/assessments/agentspex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6b3d8c0916f09f4f067aca760a92db3904ec93e\">d6b3d8c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T14:58:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/raroque/boop-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Boop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/raroque/boop-agent/tree/31979130b1371acd9defbea115279a06c63c1fb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 31979130b1371acd9defbea115279a06c63c1fb4\">3197913 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Interpret a user&#39;s message or a previously specified automation task and produce a context-sensitive answer, research/tool-mediated result, or staged external action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse message intents, incomplete task context, integration returns and failures, action confirmation and changing tool observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The runtime-invoked autonomous dispatcher decides answer versus delegated work and writes the task it delegates; each execution agent chooses how to respond and which exposed tools to invoke after seeing returns. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed dispatcher/execution agents in the first-party Boop turn path. The external inference service executes cognition, but Boop actually assembles and invokes these actors; deterministic spawn, tool filtering and storage are support/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded history, model/runtime adapter, permissions/allowed-tool lists, Convex messages/logs, scoped integrations, timeouts/abort, draft persistence, cron and usage accounting. - Closure path: Incoming user message → handleUserMessage → model chooses response or spawnagent → spawnExecutionAgent returns tool/result/status → dispatcher returns user-facing reply; independently, a due configured automation starts the same execution agent and stores/notifies outcome. - Boundary reachability: Both handleUserMessage and spawnExecutionAgent are imported by shipped server routes/scheduler. The dispatcher has an actual model-runtime call and first-party spawnagent callback, not merely a README proposal. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model-backed actor leaves webhook delivery, a database, static schedules and tools but no discretionary interpretation of arbitrary user goals or next tool selection. The developer and user set the operating envelope; the model-backed actor owns ordinary in-envelope task choices. - Evidence: model-selected dispatcher tools and result; tool-bearing worker and return; runtime provider selection. - Basis: Structural first-party assembly of a model-to-tool-to-result loop; explicit packaged operating mode. - Confidence: High for code-path existence; no claim of empirical agent effectiveness or verified vendor integration. - Caveats: This is an application orchestration of installed Claude/Codex runtime agents, not first-party inference weights or ownership of the external SDK&#39;s independent internal architecture. The shipped Boop dispatcher/worker integration is the credited loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately established first-party regulation of interference/oscillation among distinct operational work cells at the Boop service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent delegated tasks might conflict over external accounts, memory or repeated notifications; such possibility alone is not an observed S2 witness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No source-shipped conflict judgment/negotiation or returned inter-S1 adjustment distinguished from parent-to-child delegation, tool scope and message deduplication. - Decision owner: Not established. A single dispatcher assigns tasks; user approval applies to external drafts, not general inter-worker coordination. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-agent Convex logs/status, tool-scope filtering, Sendblue/webhook dedup, scheduled runs, draft status, shared memory and worker abort. - Closure path: Generic execution results return to the dispatcher; the reviewed code does not feed a specific inter-S1 conflict resolution into the next actions of two independently operating work cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model task delegation and static duplication gates do not become S2 because two work cells can exist or a message can be sent twice. - Evidence: dispatcher spawn; independent scheduled agent runs; proactive email delivery dedup. - Basis: Bounded negative, source-first functional search. - Confidence: Medium-high for the reviewed standard runtime; no assertion about downstream private integrations. - Caveats: External backends may impose locks/quotas and operators can manually coordinate tasks; neither establishes a Boop-owned inter-S1 conflict closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current organization-wide discretionary control of resources, priorities or commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale jobs, worker failures, usage growth and concurrent tasks can threaten the active service, but packaged responses are local lifecycle enforcement or human-selected settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Whole-system reallocation or intervention judgment is not supplied. Abort/retry applies to an individual worker; stale-heartbeat failure marking is deterministic. - Decision owner: User/operator for runtime configuration and manual task intervention; code for fixed stale-state and scheduling rules. No autonomous or first-party complete S3 parent-control loop is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent state dashboard, token/usage summaries, abort controllers, configured model, tool whitelist, heartbeat sweep and retry endpoint. - Closure path: A failed/stale work cell can be marked failed, retried or cancelled locally, but the observed path does not choose/revise current whole-system commitments based on a current global view. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A dashboard plus per-agent lifecycle API is observation/enforcement, not a managerial decision right over the whole; the dispatcher selects work for a user request, not S3 across active operations. - Evidence: stale-heartbeat sweep; worker cancellation/retry; server endpoints; dashboard metrics. - Basis: Bounded negative under first-party standard modes. - Confidence: Medium; operator intervention rights exist but are not shown as whole-current S3 governance. - Caveats: Operator self-hosting or dashboard administration is not by itself an evidenced S3 P mode at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: The reviewed service has quality/checking and memory consolidation, but no sufficiently independent complementary audit of ordinary S1 claims feeding a separate current-control path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect agent outputs and erroneous stored memories could distort future replies; memory maintenance checks a proposed rewrite, not a separately sourced audit of overall agent operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A memory-proposer/adversary/judge sequence does make a model judgment over merge/prune proposals; it is not presented as an independent audit of S1 reports/actual external results at the assessed service recursion. - Decision owner: The model-backed judge for the narrow memory-maintenance operation; no evidenced complementary system-level S3 audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Memory snapshots, proposer/adversary prompts, JSON verdict parse, Convex memory mutations, agent logs and ordinary tool traces. - Closure path: Judge-approved memory edits return to memory state, not as audit findings to a distinct current-control owner that can correct S1&#39;s operational assertions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple roles and an adversarial prompt do not prove an independent complementary organizational channel; all stages work on the same memory-proposal payload and shared original memory list. - Evidence: proposer/adversary/judge and memory apply; ordinary agent logging. - Basis: Structural negative at declared recursion; a narrower memory-maintenance criticism path does exist. - Confidence: Medium, specifically limited by the chosen service-level organizational boundary. - Caveats: The consolidation judge is not ignored; its evidenced judgment is credited to memory maintenance, not automatically S3 complementary access to external operational reality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party outside/future sensing → adaptation-option judgment → update of Boop&#39;s present operational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing provider capabilities, external user needs and integration landscapes might demand future adaptation, but the shipped routines react to current events/tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The proactive classifier judges whether a current email merits notifying the user; memory consolidation revises stored context; neither owns an externally prospective capability-adaptation choice. - Decision owner: Operator configures provider/integrations and upgrades the app; ordinary model work cells reason about present user tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gmail rubric, preference memory, cron, local memory decay/consolidation, runtime/model selection UI and installed integration catalog. - Closure path: Current emails may trigger messages; memory edits affect later prompt context, but no prospective environmental model develops an adaptation option and returns it to operational capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Proactive alerts are external event reactions; storing corrected memories is not a future-oriented capability redesign; runtime switching is user configuration. - Evidence: proactive classifier; memory consolidation; runtime settings. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can task an execution agent to research the future, but an arbitrary S1 research output is not an installed harness S4 adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate-identity/policy dispute and legitimate authority return path is established for the personal-agent service. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sending messages or accessing sensitive accounts requires limits, but these are ordinary action permissions and configured tool safety rather than a demonstrated S5 deliberation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user confirms a draft&#39;s ordinary external action; the dispatcher is restricted to spawn/memory/automation tools. No service-level identity change or ultimate-policy deliberation is routed and returned. - Decision owner: User/operator controls service purpose, credentials, settings and action confirmation externally. The model-backed dispatcher owns bounded S1 task choices, not ultimate identity rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Draft staging, senddraft/reject flow, allowed/disallowed tools, browser setting and isolated Codex read-only mode. - Closure path: Confirmation leads to a worker executing a pending draft; settings alter runtime options. Neither is identity/ultimate-policy question → legitimate parent decision → binding return to future operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A prompt role, static tool restriction, local runtime switch or user saying &quot;send it&quot; does not amount to S5 even when security-relevant. - Evidence: draft confirmation and commit; dispatcher allow/deny list; Codex isolation/config. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator governance and software-project maintainer policy remain real but are not part of an evidenced operational S5 parent return loop of the assessed service.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1d27f0bee31bcf3379c314e30cc8e91b212b7e02/assessments/bo/boop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1d27f0bee31bcf3379c314e30cc8e91b212b7e02\">1d27f0b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T18:46:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/threatclaw/threatclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ThreatClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/threatclaw/threatclaw/tree/71540e5da1fc4dd5ff62c37842de25efab67a3f1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 71540e5da1fc4dd5ff62c37842de25efab67a3f1\">71540e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous bounded model-based incident investigation and evidence/tool selection with live feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown incidents, evolving signals, noisy security telemetry, ambiguity about CVE/alert severity, missing evidence and failing skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured first-party model-driven investigation decides whether more context is needed and names investigation skills/parameters; loop reinvokes model with executed skill results and returns a verdict. Distinct scheduled ReAct cycle also chooses model-driven threat correlation/actions. - Decision owner: ThreatClaw-owned agent invocation and its configured model reasoning, with bounded prompts and tool dispatch; not an externally authored vendor agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dossier, preflight enrichment, skill registry, timeouts, max iterations/max calls, typed response parser, security mode and safe-action whitelist. - Closure path: asset dossier → model suspicion/skill request → first-party bounded skill execution → returned observation in following prompt → revised verdict/action proposal → recorded incident. - Boundary reachability: Booted Intelligence ticker can launch runinvestigation from selected current asset, with semaphore and incident store; runreactcycle also has an installed ticker route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides actual investigative steps based on in-run observations rather than consuming only fixed rule tables; default preflight is deterministic and does not remove model discretion on subsequent skills. - Evidence: src/agent/investigation.rs; src/agent/reactrunner.rs; src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/agent/investigationskills.rs. - Basis: Explicit reachable source-level model/skill/feedback loop. - Confidence: High for the implemented configured model path; no live external inference or detection quality measured. - Caveats: Operational mode and backend availability matter; Analyst mode disables ReAct, and malicious output is constrained by a separate guard.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent harmful concurrency and oscillation between distinct current incident/skill/model operations competing for one finite inference/worker resource or repeated asset claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: CPU-served Ollama cannot safely process unbounded concurrent investigations; simultaneous tasks could overload model service or duplicate work on a single incident/asset. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party semaphore, atomic claim, queue capacity and cooldown rules determine when other in-flight S1 investigations may proceed; model does not choose conflict mitigation discretion. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party worker-pool/Intelligence admission and suppression paths under operator-provided limits. - Boundary reachability: Standard service starts the Intelligence ticker and installed task pools; Intelligence spawns incident investigations under the global semaphore, the active registry checks for already investigated assets, and queue supervisors atomically claim and execute work. Their effects gate actual future agent investigation, not an unreachable utility module. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: INVESTIGATIONSEMAPHORE with one permit across investigation requests, WorkerPool permits across task-kind workloads, atomic claimnexttask, retry-on-fail, asset dedup registry and versioned cooldown. - Closure path: competing S1 submissions/active investigation status → permit or dedup/queue decision → task blocks/skips or is admitted → completion frees permit and updates task/asset state → subsequent S1 attempt changes. - Distinct S1 units: Separately launched dossier investigation tasks and graph/LLM/skill workloads; each can execute model/tool-bearing incident operations. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous calls to scarce one-at-a-time CPU LLM and repeated evaluations of the same incident degrade reliability, create feedback storms and compete for active current security monitoring. - Attenuating coordination relation: One-permit serialized model investigations, atomic claim/worker capacity, per-asset cooling and load-based admission. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Semaphores release on completion; verdict/error cooldowns are written back and gate future investigations; queued tasks enter running/done/failed states. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: These paths regulate concrete concurrency collisions and duplicate incident investigation, beyond data transport or merely sequencing a single authored workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A statically configured bounded structure enforces noninterference; it is S2=C, not an agent self-negotiating arbitration. - Evidence: src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/agent/taskqueue/pool.rs; src/agent/taskqueue/supervisor.rs; src/agent/taskqueue/backpressure.rs. - Basis: Structural default operating concurrency and source-evidenced failure mode; even if a particular incident pair has not been reproduced in this review. - Confidence: High for named shared LLM semaphore and atomic worker claims. - Caveats: Does not prove distributed multi-replica safety or all races are eliminated; the asset dedup cache is in-memory.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Current whole-installation prioritization, admission and escalation of monitored asset investigations from a global security situation and finite investigation capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Many simultaneous findings/alerts, competing asset risks, finite investigation throughput, alert fatigue and high-severity attack needing faster monitoring. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party scoring/threshold scheduler reads the combined current asset picture, changes polling interval, selects the highest-risk monitored asset, starts/defers incident work, suppresses predictive-only/duplicate/unowned targets, and can propose HITL response. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party Intelligence Engine plus configured rules/operator mode. The incident model supplies conclusions but does not own whole-installation resource allocation. - Boundary reachability: The production startbackgroundservices launcher starts spawnintelligenceticker; the ticker awaits runintelligencecycle and uses the resulting global SecuritySituation to choose a next interval, highest-priority asset, inventory/dedup eligibility, incident creation and real runinvestigation before the next cycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SecuritySituation aggregation, decidenotificationlevel, sorted/maximum risk asset selection, asset inventory gate, dedup/active registry, dynamic interval control, spawned investigation and remediation guard. - Closure path: whole-installation findings/alerts/graph view → asset score and alert/priority selection → actual incident investigation/deferral with capacity guard → stored verdict/result → updated next-cycle current situation and scheduling. - Whole-system current view: Aggregated findings/alerts grouped by canonical monitored asset, global score, attack flags and current investigation registry across the single SOC installation. - Current-control decision scope: Present monitoring cadence, which asset is eligible for action now, when incident investigation is admitted, how duplicate/noise work is suppressed, and when the SOC operator receives an escalation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive selection is expressed in first-party rules (maxby, thresholds, cooldown, inventory membership), not dynamically chosen by an S3 policy model. The resident model controls a specific S1 dossier, not the overall current commitment selection; therefore S3=C. - Evidence: src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/channels/web/handlers/threatclawapi.rs; src/agent/modemanager.rs; src/agent/remediationengine.rs. - Basis: Installed reachable whole-SOC scoring/admission/intervention path. - Confidence: Medium-high for bounded current priority regulator; not asserted as full staffed-SOC governance. - Caveats: No first-party strategic budget bargaining or durable organizational project-level S3 autonomy. In default Investigator mode meaningful disruptive write remediation remains human authorized.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Challenge the model investigator&#39;s incident claims with independent structured dossier/citation/telemetry evidence and feed a binding corrective verdict in enabled Strict mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-fabricated CVEs, MITRE references or citations; confident unsupported &#39;confirmed&#39; verdicts; erroneous false-positive dismissal despite Sigma/KEV/ML corroboration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic reconcileverdictfull rules compare the model snapshot with score/Sigma/CISA KEV/graph, citation checks, actual skill-call counts, typed validation; Strict overrides the conclusion when justified. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic reconciliation and separately computed corroborating signals, not another model auditor. Operator chooses Strict, Lenient or Off configuration. - Boundary reachability: The actual incident runinvestigation path parses the model answer, builds validators/citation report from stored dossier and evidence, invokes verdictreconciler::reconcileverdictfull and, whenever Strict outcome.apply is true, overwrites parsed verdict, severity and confidence before final incident state is emitted. This is not only an isolated unit test. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed validators, citation tracker, SignalsSnapshot, rule  adjustments and investigation result assembly. - Closure path: model-issued verdict → alternative dossier/graph/ML/Sigma/citation/skill evidence and validation → reconciler chooses challenge → under Strict, revised severity/verdict/confidence overwrites parsed → final incident classification/escalation; discrepancies logged. - Claim being audited: Whether the LLM&#39;s reported incident verdict and supporting evidence are credible enough to drive severity/escalation. - Ordinary reporting path: Model-issued parsed.verdict, severity, confidence and narrative from iterative S1 investigation. - Complementary access path: First-party independent deterministic alert/risk context, KEV presence, graph context, citation pointers verified against real dossier, actual executed-skill counter and typed structural checks. - Independence boundary: Materially different evidence and rules compared to LLM self-judgment; not a separate human/reviewer deployment. The evidence is provided by the same installation and may be incomplete; rules skip missing graph context and do not establish independent source provenance for every field. - Who acts on findings: runinvestigation applies outcome.log.reconciled to its live parsed verdict only if outcome.apply (Strict); that final verdict feeds incident outcome. Lenient only logs and Off disables. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Rules bind correction from complementary data but no independent LLM agent chooses it, so S3=C, not S3=A. - Evidence: src/agent/investigation.rs; src/agent/verdictreconciler.rs; src/agent/evidencetracker.rs; src/agent/validators/mod.rs; src/agent/validationmode.rs. - Basis: Explicit source-attested alternative-evidence challenge in actual investigation path, binding only under Strict mode. - Confidence: High for existence of Strict-mode override; limited/conditional as audit coverage. - Caveats: Default validation mode must not be assumed strict without checking runtime config; this is a supported conditional C path, not a mandatory protected independent auditor. An internal source bug could contaminate both the model dossier and verifier input.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supported first-party strategic/prospective organizational adaptation decision with a two-way return to S3 established at this revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing new IoCs, Sigma/MITRE/KEV feeds, future threats and feature/vendor rule updates are externally relevant, but ingestion alone does not prove S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The optional updater follows an external supplier&#39;s signed pack manifest/version and installs verified bytes; it does not evaluate future alternatives, select a capability-investment/adaptation option or negotiate it with current control. Operational thresholds are prewritten rules. - Decision owner: External supplier for package contents/version, administrator for support key/enablement, deterministic first-party signature/hash/version synchronizer for installation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: 6-hour updater schedule, signed-manifest verification, per-pack registry, anti-rollback and verified install, enrichment periodic resync. - Closure path: External feed → validated data/rule pack → current detection configuration may change, but no first-party evidenced prospective option synthesis/selection → S3 dialogue → organizational adaptation decision/feedback cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic update is a supply synchronization mechanism, not an autonomous organization-level S4 regulator merely because it ingests evolving threats. - Evidence: src/agent/ruleupdater.rs; src/agent/intelligenceengine.rs; src/agent/sigmaengine.rs; src/agent/modemanager.rs. - Basis: Bounded source review of strongest apparent candidate, signed pack updater, plus enrichment and mission/planning alternatives. - Confidence: Medium; would change with explicit documented first-party adaptive option/test/apply path. - Caveats: Operational detection improvement is possible through new signatures; the negative state is about missing S4 organizational function, not inability to update rules.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party decisive ultimate identity/ethos/policy-setting and returned governance loop shown for this deployed SOC installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model safety, operator action privileges, immutable-soul tampering and tool permission boundaries are regulated by static configuration and enforced action checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Project-supplied AGENTSOUL.toml is hash-verified, while the operator sets autonomy mode/approval. Neither a live first-party final-policy conflict adjudicator nor binding identity-policy adaptation/return is shown. - Decision owner: External project developer and site SOC administrator configure fixed identity and operational constraints; no dedicated S5 in assessed first-party runtime proven. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Compile-time soul hash, immutablerules prompt injection, mode settings, whitelist, nonce/atomic approvals, protected infrastructure guard. - Closure path: Static rule check or human consent gates a current command, but no first-party ultimate issue → responsible higher-level governing decision → identity/policy settlement → returned organizational response loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static &#39;soul&#39; data, boot hash and human approval do not themselves create Beer S5; S1 can propose actions but cannot redefine ultimate system identity. - Evidence: src/agent/soul.rs; AGENTSOUL.toml; src/agent/modemanager.rs; src/agent/remediationengine.rs; src/agent/remediationguard.rs. - Basis: Bounded negative analysis of first-party identity, security and approval surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high for reviewed operating scope. - Caveats: A customer&#39;s actual security policy and supplier stewardship are separate organizations; don&#39;t inherit them into the installed software automatically.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cca7c7d47ae0afc940a1f68ff3bea6a71287cef8/assessments/threatclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cca7c7d47ae0afc940a1f68ff3bea6a71287cef8\">cca7c7d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T16:31:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/harnessclaw/harnessclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">harnessclaw/harnessclaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/harnessclaw/harnessclaw/tree/44d01c1fa2193d1d4f479327cc15cf1bec09851c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 44d01c1fa2193d1d4f479327cc15cf1bec09851c\">44d01c1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform model-selected tool and delegation actions to address user tasks within a supported desktop agent session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user task instructions, tool/browser/file outputs and provider or subagent responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the native engine&#39;s LLM loop selects tool calls and continuation; desktop user gates particular actions. - Decision owner: model-driven Harnessclaw Engine agent within the first-party distributed desktop/runtime composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawned native engine, WebSocket session/card transport, permission requests, tool-result return, persisted session forest and provider configuration. - Closure path: user/session message → engine model/tool cycle → tool or delegated result → engine continuation/finish → streamed cards and session result into desktop UI. - Boundary reachability: release workflow bundles a real platform engine binary, Electron resolves and spawns it, and client starts authenticated sessions; engine tag v0.0.22 provides first-party source evidence for the observed distribution path. Frontend shell alone is not the claimed agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: engine model/tool selection is discretionary, unlike static WebSocket card parsing or Electron launch code. - Evidence: packaged binary resolution and process, process spawn, engine query-loop README. - Basis: structural supported packaged-mode wiring and a named observed engine release; not a live provider execution. - Confidence: medium for stable source-to-binary correspondence given frontend&#39;s non-pinned runtime selector; high that a supported installed agentic mode is intentionally shipped. - Caveats: do not generalize to unknown future engine assets, or treat mock/demo/event translation as a substitute for actual agent execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party S2-specific inter-S1 disturbance attenuation and returned adjustment is evidenced in the bounded available runtime path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: subagent scheduling, card visibility and ordered task dispatch are generic work management until a concrete cross-worker collision/relation is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent may dispatch subagents; deterministic scheduler selects sync/async execution strategy, but does not demonstrate an inter-unit conflict resolution decision. - Decision owner: native parent agent for delegation, scheduler for dispatch, not a distinct S2 attenuation actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent cards, event streams, scheduler Dispatch/Subscribe, tool permissions. - Closure path: subagent tool requests and results return to the parent agent; no evidenced collision signal changes a second worker&#39;s subsequent operating conduct. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple workers and ordering are not, by themselves, viable inter-S1 coordination. - Evidence: client subagent event handling, engine scheduler contract. - Basis: reviewed supported frontend and tagged runtime source. - Confidence: medium; current release selection is not immutable in this frontend ref. - Caveats: a future coordinator with concrete inter-S1 collision control would need a new evidence-specific assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no proven whole-system current resource/commitment control loop beyond user configuration, individual approvals and delegated task management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, session failures, step retry/replan budgets, runtime restart and task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user can approve a local tool or answer a plan/step prompt; engine controls per-turn limits and task dispatch. - Decision owner: user for local approvals, native model for operational task decisions, static engine for dispatch/limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, step-decision WebSocket bookkeeping, session reconnect, scheduler tool. - Closure path: UI replies unblock a pending individual tool/step; no demonstrated view/regulation of shared current whole-system commitments with a legitimate parent S3 decision loop. Tagged runtime&#39;s plan-coordinator tool mode returns unavailable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an &#39;agent team&#39; UI and a human gate are insufficient without a whole-system current-control decision owner and returned resource intervention. - Evidence: step/plan UI response, runtime explicit unavailable plan mode. - Basis: structural, release-bounded. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: re-evaluate after engine coordinator restoration and explicit version pin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently owned complementary operational check of agent results is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emitted tool/card summaries, developer diagnostics and prompt validation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: same runtime emits progress; user may inspect outcomes; no distinct audit owner with alternative access channel. - Decision owner: none in the installed task operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event log, card forest, error reports and tests. - Closure path: ordinary execution telemetry is displayed but independent findings are not returned into a corrective management decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: event viewing and user approvals lack a separate auditor and independent evidentiary path. - Evidence: stream/card conversion, session logs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for observed runtime. - Caveats: manual inspection by user is not credited as independent S3 agent authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective environment-scanning and capability-adaptation loop beyond model task planning or maintainer releases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new skills/models/providers, application updates and future tools. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/maintainers install skills, configure model settings and ship releases. - Decision owner: adjacent human user/developer, not a first-party future-intelligence actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills market, engine config, updater, release pipeline. - Closure path: configuration/release can change future sessions; no autonomous outside-and-then option-development decision visibly returns to current operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning an assigned coding task is S1; installing a skill is a user action, not system-level S4. - Evidence: skills market, runtime and update code. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a later engine release may add such a loop; the front-end selector is not pinned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-purpose or organizational identity decision loop at one desktop agent-session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, tool allow/deny, user questions and agent personas. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user sets tool permissions and responds to individual approvals; engine applies rules. - Decision owner: task user and deterministic enforcement, not a designated S5 ultimate-authority mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool allow/deny, session user prompts, profile/skill settings. - Closure path: decisions unlock specific tool or conversation operations; no identity-policy dispute returns to govern S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission is operational risk control, not identity-level policy/constitutional legitimacy. - Evidence: permission client, permissions at engine tag. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: software community governance is outside this one-session system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2edb9e43f23b0390ac188bdf9730d996e3075dc2/assessments/ha/harnessclaw-harnessclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2edb9e43f23b0390ac188bdf9730d996e3075dc2\">2edb9e4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-07T01:50:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maziluiosif/oxi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Oxi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-07\">2026-11-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maziluiosif/oxi/tree/da693ad021667ce2a2360182f572bb73b6812add\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision da693ad021667ce2a2360182f572bb73b6812add\">da693ad &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding, source investigation and bounded workspace operations selected by the model against real tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete task descriptions, source ambiguity, unknown symbols, failing commands, provider transport errors, permissions, workspace state changes and context limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured LLM decides which first-party read/search/edit/shell/Git/web tool to call, how to change code, what failure to investigate and how to proceed after the tool returns. - Decision owner: native Oxi model-driven tool loop; first-party Rust orchestrates dispatch, context and effect restrictions but not semantic code editing choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagentrun, built-in provider-specific loops, ToolEnv, file/path/shell tools, MCP transport, workspace context, session store and ApprovalGate. - Closure path: user coding goal → native model tool request → first-party effect after applicable approval → returned command/file observation or refusal → model correction/retry or final response. - Boundary reachability: the non-ACP spawnagentrun path builds native tool definitions, history and ToolEnv before calling runchatloop/runanthropicloop/runcodexresponsesloop; these are supplied by the shipped executable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model goal-directed selection leaves a versatile but passive code editor and tools; only the model decides which actions solve the user&#39;s coding objective. - Evidence: native runner, tool registry, provider loop. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the native code path. - Caveats: no real inference/model-backed source-edit run was executed; ACP-only deployments must be assessed at the external agent&#39;s own boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party anti-interference loop among distinct simultaneously operating S1 units is evidenced in the native Oxi coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: separate chats/workspaces, cancellation, approval waits and GUI/source-file updates affect one operating session but do not constitute a concrete inter-S1 collision protocol. - Distinct S1 units: the native loop serves one coding goal per run; concurrent local chats are isolated workspace histories, not a shipped collaborating multi-worker team with shared commitments. - Inter-S1 disturbance: no specific competing model-operated native S1 writers/readers or oscillating commitments are structurally coupled by a first-party coordination relation. - Attenuating coordination relation: not applicable; session storage, tool order and GUI synchronization regulate one run or display, not cross-S1 collisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: tool results and approvals feed the current S1, without a returned inter-unit conflict/settlement signal. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a provider option or one-at-a-time tool dispatcher does not supply two interfering operating units; external ACP agent coordination stays outside the owned boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party native inter-S1 conflict choice. - Decision owner: none evidenced; sequential tool handling and UI display are coded support for S1. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session history, UI events, native model tool loop and per-action approvals. - Closure path: local tool outcome → same coding model; no competing S1 feedback arbitration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no first-party team or inter-unit disagreement selection exists beyond the external ACP delegation boundary. - Evidence: runner including ACP split, session histories, product modes. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosting other agents under ACP does not confer their internal S2 rights on Oxi.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned or constructor-owned whole-current resource/commitment manager across multiple native S1 operational units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool-round ceilings, timeouts, provider changes and permission denials bound current operations but are per-run reliability or user setup decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct whole-run allocation/prioritization/exception intervention across a portfolio of operating S1 cells; the model selects task actions and the user can stop/reconfigure its run. - Decision owner: user for task/model/settings/approval; current S1 model for its task; fixed Rust for execution limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxtoolrounds, context history compaction, provider switch and cancellation, local model/SSH management, session UI and approvals. - Closure path: a failed command returns to its own model; human can cancel or switch providers, without a first-party whole-current S3 policy and returned multi-unit reallocation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a running user task and hard watchdog do not make S3 merely because their execution is managed by a Tokio runtime. - Evidence: native execution and limits, loop parameters, approval right. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: manual workspace/user scheduling is external human operation, not a first-party parent-mode S3 at a clearly established multi-unit recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate complementary evidence path with sufficiently independent direct audit judgment and returned corrective control was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: commands can be run to test edits and the UI shows diffs, but these normally remain self-selected operational feedback or human inspection rather than an independent audit path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: native Oxi does not ship a distinct read-only reviewer agent, independent verifier or raw-source contradiction gate over the producing model&#39;s completion report. - Decision owner: same operating model can voluntarily run checks; user can inspect editor/Git panes, but no internal independent audit judgment is returned to current agent control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bash, gitdiff, file reads, undo journal, UI diff display and existing session result logs. - Claim being audited: potential claim that a coding edit succeeded; no separate first-party actor is wired to challenge it after production. - Ordinary reporting path: same model&#39;s chosen commands, tool results and final assistant answer. - Complementary access path: GUI diff/terminal display is available to the user, but not a separate first-party audit feedback decision and return route to the coding model. - Independence boundary: ordinary verification commands are invoked in the same production context; screen display and tests as development assets are not a separate audit organization. - Who acts on findings: operating model reacts to its own tool results, or human manually reviews; no distinct audit finding-to-control loop shown. - Closure path: model observes testing/shell response and may repair; independent S3 verdict absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model checking its own output or a GUI showing diffs does not constitute independently grounded challenge of routine production reporting. - Evidence: first-party tools, agent loop, diff UI. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external ACP agents may expose their own audits, which are explicitly not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective external-environment modelling, future adaptation-option selection and controlled return into coding capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: present model availability, future model downloads, workspace task changes, local hardware resources and web search may influence existing operations but are not assembled into a forward-looking adaptation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human selects providers/prompts; source updates are developer work; no supported S4 actor decides prospective changes to the agent&#39;s future policy/skills/tools as a function of sensed external trends. - Decision owner: user for model/download/settings choice; model for immediate task/web research; external maintainers for product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local GGUF model installer, SSH compute tunnel, provider catalog, keyless search, editable system prompt and software updater. - Closure path: user picks a presently available model or agent uses web search → current task receives more capability/information. No future-option model, strategy judgement and returned new operational capability loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: search, model switching, general learning and updating a binary are insufficient for S4 without external-and-prospective option creation and closure. - Evidence: product options, local-model management, web tool, updater. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: manually selecting a stronger provider for future use is a user preference, not sufficient evidence of a separate adaptive S4 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision path and returned binding organizational charter closure is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe shell commands, out-of-scope paths, MCP side effects and user tool consent are effect-risk controls rather than disputes over Oxi&#39;s organizational mission or ethos. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity- or ultimate-policy issue traverses an authorized parent deliberation/ratification path and returns as a durable direction governing subsequent agent operation. - Decision owner: human user owns per-command approval and existing application settings; constructor owns hardcoded limits; neither is demonstrated as a function-specific S5 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ApprovalPolicy, ApprovalGate, optional ApproveRest, disabled/risky tools, path fences, user prompt and settings. - Closure path: requested mutating tool → user approves/denies → result returned to same model; no mission-level policy change/return is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: capability restriction and static guardrails do not themselves choose ultimate purpose; an operator confirming an edit is not ratification of a new organizational identity. - Evidence: approval implementation, system prompt, tool limits. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a real team&#39;s policy could govern a human using Oxi externally, but is not a shipped first-party closed S5 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cb57c98b0a1c1af1d1a7a0bc5122e461d1a1fa76/assessments/ox/oxi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cb57c98b0a1c1af1d1a7a0bc5122e461d1a1fa76\">cb57c98 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T16:36:30+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Rustam-Z/hamroh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hamroh</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Rustam-Z/hamroh/tree/0970d3a3dc8ff50970998515afe4ef79510f397e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0970d3a3dc8ff50970998515afe4ef79510f397e\">0970d3a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform conversational, retrieval, memory and scheduled assistant work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing requests, external observations, chat context, tool failures and incomplete long tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tools, interpret their results, send/withhold an answer and continue long work through heartbeat. - Decision owner: configured model-backed Claude agent running as the deployment&#39;s S1 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Engine buffering, worker stream transport, local MCP tools, SQLite/memory, tool allowlists and failure recovery. - Closure path: inbound messages/reminders reach worker.send; agent tool results and further messages drive subsequent decisions; structured turn results return to Engine and user-facing tools deliver outcomes. - Boundary reachability: startup builds the shipped worker/tool/prompt configuration and CcWorker.start launches that operational actor. This credits the assembled Hamroh mode, not uninspected Claude governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hamroh&#39;s transport alone cannot choose task-dependent tool calls or useful replies; those choices are made by its configured agent. - Evidence: hamroh/startup.py; hamroh/ccworker/spec.py; hamroh/ccworker/worker.py; hamroh/engine/engine.py; prompts/system.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Claude Code/model service is a required external implementation dependency; no live provider execution or inherited higher-function claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped function-specific inter-S1 interference regulation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one shared conversation engine serializes ordinary turns; optional subagents perform delegated tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no specific cross-unit conflict-resolution right is packaged. - Decision owner: parent agent chooses subtask scope; developer selects queue/tool restrictions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: single-turn buffer, reminder row claims, shared tool surface and read-only-by-default subagent guidance. - Closure path: child results return as task data; reminder callbacks regulate delivery lifecycle, not distinct operational units&#39; mutual adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: message sequencing, duplicate-reminder prevention and generic delegation safety are not an S2-specific organizational path. - Evidence: hamroh/engine/engine.py; hamroh/scheduler/reminderscheduler.py; prompts/subagents.md; plugins.json.example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The shared-identity destructive-write warning is real exposure, but no actual inter-unit conflict/oscillation and corrective coordination relation is reconstructed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: owner-governed current regulation of the whole installed assistant&#39;s admission and shared execution continuity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long/stuck shared turns, growing inbound queue, degraded delivery and continuing commitments across authorized chats. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret global operational state and choose pause/resume of inbound operations or replacement of the shared execution session. - Decision owner: authenticated deployment owner identified by HAMROHOWNERID. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: health readout, owner-ID command guard, dispatcher paused gate, worker reset/restore and status replies. - Whole-system current view: health combines current engine duration/queue with deployment-wide last send, reflection reminder status and rate-limit notices; audit/log views add failures and storage changes. - Current-control decision scope: all Telegram inbound admission for this bot and the common Claude session; not per-tool approval and not a claim that pause also stops the reminder scheduler. - Closure path: owner observes current readout and selects a command; pause/resume changes dispatcher.onmessage handling, and resetsession restarts the shared worker with restore context. Subsequent operations use that returned state. - Boundary reachability: registered owner commands are direct runtime handlers available in the documented self-hosted mode, without composing a new supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the owner exercises current intervention discretion; deterministic worker recovery, health transport and command enforcement do not own that judgment. - Evidence: hamroh/telegramio/commands.py; hamroh/telegramio/dispatcher.py; hamroh/engine/engine.py; hamroh/ccworker/worker.py; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Parent mode is a supported human control loop, not evidence of autonomous S3, a recorded intervention trace, or every kill/permission command being S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit judgment with returned corrective control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the same assistant reviews prior outputs and owners can request diagnostic readouts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reflection judgment belongs to the producing assistant; /audit formats failures/backups/footprint without a separate audit mandate/judgment loop. - Decision owner: same operational agent for self-review; owner for subsequent supervision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: raw message/reaction database, /audit and /logs, backups and reflection reasoning records. - Closure path: self-review proposals enter owner adaptation/policy decisions; deterministic diagnostics expose data but do not install independent audit ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-reflection or a command named audit is not sufficiently independent complementary audit. - Evidence: skills/self-reflection/SKILL.md; hamroh/telegramio/commands.py; hamroh/tools/databasequery.py; prompts/system.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Optional child isolation is task-context isolation; it does not by itself supply an independent auditor or different operational access.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: owner-governed prospective adaptation of assistant behavior/procedures to its user environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, negative reactions, language/tone mismatches and rules that would misfire in future situations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve, reject, refine or redirect the proposed capability/behavior change after scenario testing. - Decision owner: deployment owner; agent owns investigation, scenario generation and proposal, not final adoption. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: protected reminder seed, skill loader, SQL/memory access, reflection log, skill/instruction write tools and restart/reload. - External distinction: actual Telegram user corrections/reactions and observed mismatches with user/group expectations, rather than internal memory growth alone. - Future / prospective distinction: each candidate is tested on 10–20 on-target, adjacent, off-target and boundary scenarios to anticipate later misapplication. - Adaptation option generated: proposed refined standing rule, reusable skill/playbook or environment-specific memory update with an explicit proposed destination. - Path back into current capability / S3: owner reply selects the option/target; agent writes the approved skill or rule; current tools read memory/skills and owner restarts the worker to load modified overlay/index. - Closure path: reflection steps 4–6 route options to the owner, parse the returned decision and execute only approved changes; step 7 states activation/restart and rollback. Subsequent work uses the changed procedure/instructions. - Boundary reachability: startup auto-seeds the packaged skill; scheduler injects a real reminder; plugins.toolgroups.subagents=true exposes the Agent tool required by the heavy-skill prompt. This is a supported first-party flag, not custom authority wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without owner adoption the proposals remain pending; agent scenario reasoning and storage machinery do not own the adaptation decision. - Evidence: skills/self-reflection/SKILL.md; prompts/system.md; hamroh/startup.py; hamroh/scheduler/reminderscheduler.py; hamroh/tools/skills.py; hamroh/tools/instructions.py; hamroh/ccworker/spec.py; docs/tools.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S4=P is for the supported subagents-enabled reflection mode. Default example disables Agent despite prompt-required background spawning; no successful default-mode reflection run is asserted. Write authorization is prompt-enforced and restart is human-owned.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: parent-governed deployment identity and durable operating-policy closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposed changes to bot persona, language/ethos and session-wide standing behavior, including policy proposals produced by reflection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the legitimate owner chooses which standing identity/behavior rule governs this bot and may dictate wording or reject it. - Decision owner: deployment owner for ultimate policy within the parent-supplied immutable base constraints; no agent-owned ultimate authority is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner metadata instructions, project overlay read/append/rewrite, backups, size/path restrictions and fresh-spawn prompt composition. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether to change the bot&#39;s identity/personality or a durable behavior default for every session, not whether one ordinary tool call may proceed. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: HAMROHOWNERID holder governs the installed bot&#39;s project overlay; base system.md and host-level enablement remain higher/developer constraints. - Return-to-operation path: owner request or approved/refined reflection proposal → first-party instruction tools update project.md → owner restart/new spawn → composesystemprompt concatenates the returned identity/policy overlay for subsequent operation. - Closure path: documented owner persona edits and rule adoption are persisted with backups; the worker loads that decision on the next spawn, rather than merely displaying a policy file. - Boundary reachability: the shipped project example explicitly holds Identity &amp; personality; system.md exposes the owner-only editing process and startup wires InstructionsStore into the local tool surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent applies the owner&#39;s standing decision; it cannot unilaterally promote the rule or alter immutable base policy. - Evidence: prompts/project.md.example; prompts/system.md; skills/self-reflection/SKILL.md; hamroh/tools/instructions.py; hamroh/storage/instructionsstore.py; hamroh/startup.py; hamroh/ccworker/spec.py; docs/documentation.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: P is bounded to the installed bot&#39;s identity/standing policy. Owner-only instruction writes rely on model policy, not a cryptographic/code authorization check; this is not a security guarantee or blanket S5 credit for access settings.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f819c48d0734987250ad11729a8cb6a7b73db509/assessments/ha/hamroh.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f819c48d0734987250ad11729a8cb6a7b73db509\">f819c48 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T21:57:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/venkatapgummadi/agentflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentFlow (venkatapgummadi)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/venkatapgummadi/agentflow/tree/81c07248082130f6528e335b24be0f1d85c8a1f6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81c07248082130f6528e335b24be0f1d85c8a1f6\">81c0724 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose a task-specific API operation plan for the user&#39;s intent and deliver it into actionable enterprise integration steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: diverse natural-language requests and API domains require different operations, dependencies, parameters and capability tags. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the semantic operations, ordered dependencies and parameters from user intent; these choices materially affect which requests are planned and executed. - Decision owner: the configured model in supported LLMIntentParser or LLM-first HybridIntentParser mode. The integrating developer chooses the model provider and permitted connector set; the deterministic planner maps model output to endpoint candidates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSON schema prompt, normalization, parse confidence/fallback, API discovery, capability matching, plan DAG, bounded parallel execution, connector invocation, circuit-breaker/retry and result journal. - Closure path: user intent → model-generated operation payload → createplan DAG → executor&#39;s connector calls → step results/validation returned to the caller; the payload changes the current action path. - Boundary reachability: the shipped orchestrator directly calls parseasync for injected LLM/hybrid parsers and passes operations to the shipped planner/executor. A documented CallableLLMProvider is the supported provider boundary; an out-of-repository model service supplies inference but no separately orchestrating host agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: replacing the model with the default parser yields only heuristic decomposition; a real selected model owns the discretionary semantic operation set in the explicitly wired mode. The later deterministic router/executor and the human&#39;s choice of connectors do not own that model choice. - Evidence: orchestrator, LLM parser, provider adapter, documented deployment, executor. - Basis: structural first-party model invocation and documented configuration. - Confidence: medium; source-path reachability is strong, external model effectiveness not tested. - Caveats: default operation is deterministic and DeterministicMockProvider is not evidence of agent autonomy. Inference is a one-shot planning pass, not a model/tool observation/replanning loop; some executor branches remain placeholders.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no established inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation among distinct credited autonomous operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent DAG calls can contend for external resources or share data dependencies; merely launching them in parallel does not establish S2. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate specific inter-unit interference and revise later operating relations. - Decision owner: no distinct S2 owner; developer-defined plan dependency and semaphore enforce scheduling. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: planner DAG dependencies, executor ready-step selection and concurrency semaphore, shared context and event journal. - Closure path: ready steps are scheduled/serialized, but no S2-specific disturbance detection → discretionary coordination response → changed later S1 behaviour is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/executor/validator are stages of one workflow, not independently autonomous sibling S1 businesses; neither a DAG edge nor a semaphore inherits a coordinating decision right. - Evidence: planner, executor, context. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: high for the reviewed standard run. - Caveats: an embedding application can assemble distinct agents and a richer S2, but that composition is not in this assessed library distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no established whole-system current-operations regulation across multiple credited S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: API failures, per-step resource consumption, endpoint health, concurrency and task completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: use a whole-system current view to revise resources, priorities, commitments or constraints for distinct operational S1 units. - Decision owner: host developer configures limits/connectors; deterministic executor/router enforces predefined per-run responses. No first-party S3 discretionary owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxparallelsteps, budget router, dynamic endpoint scores, retry/circuit breaker, event journal and per-run result envelope. - Closure path: routine execution can retry or mark failures, but no owner evaluates a portfolio-level current view and returns an authoritative new multi-S1 control arrangement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the code&#39;s AgentOrchestrator name and budget/latency calculations do not transfer the choice of shared commitments to an autonomous actor. - Evidence: orchestrator, budget router, dynamic router. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: these are useful S1 task execution controls. A business host may add supervising governance but it is not a credited first-party parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary, sufficiently independent evidence challenge to ordinary operational reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed or misleading API results, false completion and missing output fields. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect operational reality and feed findings back into control outside the routine producing/reporting path. - Decision owner: normal ValidatorAgent executes fixed rules in the same run; no independent audit owner is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: result/event journal, completion/failure/data-presence validation and user-supplied rule callbacks. - Closure path: ordinary validator returns a validation report with the result, but its path is the standard pipeline and is not a separate complementary audit with independent access and control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the validator an agent does not create independent evidence or autonomous audit judgment. - Evidence: validator, orchestrator, context. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separate operator audit using journal and raw API state could be built, but it is outside the shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no established external-and-prospective adaptation option loop returning new capability to current operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing API latency, failure rates, costs, available endpoints and future integration demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: turn external/prospective distinctions into capability alternatives, select an adaptation, and feed it back to current S3/operations. - Decision owner: developer chooses connector/model/weight strategy; score updates and adaptive weight optimization are deterministic local response algorithms, not an agent-owned future capability decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: router metric history and AdaptiveWeightOptimizer, API discovery, fallback and connector configuration. - Closure path: scores can alter endpoint preference in an embedding context, but no external future model, alternatives evaluation and S4-to-current-capability decision/return are supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: online score adjustment is current routing optimization; an LLM interpreting one user&#39;s present task is not strategic external sensing. - Evidence: adaptive optimizer, dynamic router, LLM parser. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the repo describes adaptive routing; no live SLA/tradeoff quality result was measured here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party operationally closed identity/ultimate-policy authority at the selected orchestration-run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: enterprise data-access boundaries, developer policies, model use and high-stakes API action choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue and return a decision governing subsequent operation. - Decision owner: implementing enterprise/developer chooses connectors, budgets and compliance mode outside the library; no supported in-run ultimate-policy actor is present. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider prompt/schema, deterministic validation, parser fallback, connector authentication, fixed budgets, repository policy/docs. - Closure path: static configuration constrains API execution but no first-party parent identity-policy question → legitimate authority → returned organization-wide change path is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task parsing and route choice are operational discretion, not legitimate constitution/identity setting; configuring a policy externally does not establish S5=P without closed runtime governance. - Evidence: orchestrator, hybrid parser, LLM provider boundary, security policy. - Basis: bounded negative including parent-mode inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user&#39;s enterprise may have actual higher-recursion S5, but no corresponding first-party path was observed here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c10bad4fe6e60dd9c0984a009920b62045f842f1/assessments/ag/agentflow-venkatapgummadi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c10bad4fe6e60dd9c0984a009920b62045f842f1\">c10bad4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
